[{:db/id 17592186068878 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c2c62744-4e6e-5285-9246-bd3c3e1b0773" "question-22229a6c-eb9f-551d-9867-ebd127134155"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c2c62744-4e6e-5285-9246-bd3c3e1b0773" :question/id #uuid "c2c62744-4e6e-5285-9246-bd3c3e1b0773" :question/math-academy-id "q-3131" :question/problem "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $49{j}^{2}-64=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49{j}^{2}-64 & =0 \\\\ (7j)^{2}-{8}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (7j-8)(7j+8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n7j-8=0\\,\\text{or}\\,7j+8=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7j-8 & =0 & \\, & \\, & 7j+8 & =0 \\\\ 7j & =8 & \\, & \\, & 7j & =-8 \\\\ j & =\\frac{8}{7} & \\, & \\, & j & =-\\frac{8}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $j=-\\frac{8}{7},\\frac{8}{7}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $j=\\pm \\frac{8}{7}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-20c66023-fe7d-5634-bdab-140e09291918"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-20c66023-fe7d-5634-bdab-140e09291918" :answer-field/id #uuid "20c66023-fe7d-5634-bdab-140e09291918" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bb60f34e-6211-5013-859c-8c21ceaeecc9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f694123a-9481-5f13-bad5-eb94fed69a96" "answer-7d4ea996-7e02-5985-abd5-43d8ffcc57a4" "answer-d2f41667-d9e5-5380-ba9f-472a87707f29" "answer-6c015956-8ceb-574e-b57d-7e90e9485963" "answer-bb60f34e-6211-5013-859c-8c21ceaeecc9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f694123a-9481-5f13-bad5-eb94fed69a96" :answer/id #uuid "f694123a-9481-5f13-bad5-eb94fed69a96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "j=\\pm \\frac{16}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d4ea996-7e02-5985-abd5-43d8ffcc57a4" :answer/id #uuid "7d4ea996-7e02-5985-abd5-43d8ffcc57a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "j=\\pm \\frac{4}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2f41667-d9e5-5380-ba9f-472a87707f29" :answer/id #uuid "d2f41667-d9e5-5380-ba9f-472a87707f29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "j=\\pm \\frac{7}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c015956-8ceb-574e-b57d-7e90e9485963" :answer/id #uuid "6c015956-8ceb-574e-b57d-7e90e9485963" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "j=\\pm \\frac{7}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb60f34e-6211-5013-859c-8c21ceaeecc9" :answer/id #uuid "bb60f34e-6211-5013-859c-8c21ceaeecc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "j=\\pm \\frac{8}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22229a6c-eb9f-551d-9867-ebd127134155" :question/id #uuid "22229a6c-eb9f-551d-9867-ebd127134155" :question/math-academy-id "q-12785" :question/problem "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $4{k}^{2}-36=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Before we start our normal process of factoring, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $4.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4{k}^{2}-36=4({k}^{2}-9)\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({k}^{2}-9) & =0 \\\\ 4({k}^{2}-{3}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4(k-3)(k+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nk-3=0\\,\\text{or}\\,k+3=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nk=3\\,\\text{or}\\,k=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=-3,3.$ We can write this even more concisely as $k=\\pm 3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1271505d-716e-5d6d-a0d2-c688dce6429b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1271505d-716e-5d6d-a0d2-c688dce6429b" :answer-field/id #uuid "1271505d-716e-5d6d-a0d2-c688dce6429b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5a9cd8c6-701e-5a0f-89c0-f811b41febe9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b1f70480-6564-5dbe-8439-da4c15dc9c7f" "answer-07157920-abd5-5bf2-a352-0cf1e00e0462" "answer-12e36eca-3675-577b-9ee7-798a0a376d8d" "answer-5a9cd8c6-701e-5a0f-89c0-f811b41febe9" "answer-c101184b-a1da-5275-90f3-849ec9155b99"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1f70480-6564-5dbe-8439-da4c15dc9c7f" :answer/id #uuid "b1f70480-6564-5dbe-8439-da4c15dc9c7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07157920-abd5-5bf2-a352-0cf1e00e0462" :answer/id #uuid "07157920-abd5-5bf2-a352-0cf1e00e0462" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12e36eca-3675-577b-9ee7-798a0a376d8d" :answer/id #uuid "12e36eca-3675-577b-9ee7-798a0a376d8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a9cd8c6-701e-5a0f-89c0-f811b41febe9" :answer/id #uuid "5a9cd8c6-701e-5a0f-89c0-f811b41febe9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=\\pm 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c101184b-a1da-5275-90f3-849ec9155b99" :answer/id #uuid "c101184b-a1da-5275-90f3-849ec9155b99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=\\pm 6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068879 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1bf2ec1b-375e-51fb-ba57-6d1d010ff509"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1bf2ec1b-375e-51fb-ba57-6d1d010ff509" :question/id #uuid "1bf2ec1b-375e-51fb-ba57-6d1d010ff509" :question/math-academy-id "q-23553" :question/problem "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $4{x}^{2}=25.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-25 & =0 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-(5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (2x-5)(2x+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n2x-5=0\\,\\text{or}\\,2x+5=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =0 & \\, & \\, & 2x+5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 & \\, & \\, & 2x & =-5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5}{2}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $x=\\pm \\frac{5}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e2131dde-8f57-5325-b5c9-e3e9de599f08"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e2131dde-8f57-5325-b5c9-e3e9de599f08" :answer-field/id #uuid "e2131dde-8f57-5325-b5c9-e3e9de599f08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f2b1a9b9-ef17-58b0-9243-95a3a967a631" :answer-field/choices ["answer-225758a5-6eb6-5e61-b1e2-66c86e0d5afa" "answer-1f67d2a4-0336-5ce5-9f20-b8c50799aee4" "answer-d466eb8b-ab50-5f71-b7e6-a93daf8fc8a6" "answer-f2b1a9b9-ef17-58b0-9243-95a3a967a631" "answer-1d8c45c0-f456-5018-a401-f5b8c6c0145e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-225758a5-6eb6-5e61-b1e2-66c86e0d5afa" :answer/id #uuid "225758a5-6eb6-5e61-b1e2-66c86e0d5afa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f67d2a4-0336-5ce5-9f20-b8c50799aee4" :answer/id #uuid "1f67d2a4-0336-5ce5-9f20-b8c50799aee4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d466eb8b-ab50-5f71-b7e6-a93daf8fc8a6" :answer/id #uuid "d466eb8b-ab50-5f71-b7e6-a93daf8fc8a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2b1a9b9-ef17-58b0-9243-95a3a967a631" :answer/id #uuid "f2b1a9b9-ef17-58b0-9243-95a3a967a631" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d8c45c0-f456-5018-a401-f5b8c6c0145e" :answer/id #uuid "1d8c45c0-f456-5018-a401-f5b8c6c0145e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a332065b-8d89-5ece-8df1-c8721027cb7f" :activity/id #uuid "a332065b-8d89-5ece-8df1-c8721027cb7f" :activity/title "Solving Quadratic Equations Using a Difference of Squares" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048157 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-2b77aa42-833d-5567-ba4d-f232dd423a6b" "step-f184aec9-5e96-5e74-9416-ea4e8754a3ed" "step-847fa0d2-e5ef-5701-9540-0f72c70df40e"] :activity/first-step "step-2b77aa42-833d-5567-ba4d-f232dd423a6b" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-2b77aa42-833d-5567-ba4d-f232dd423a6b" :step/id #uuid "2b77aa42-833d-5567-ba4d-f232dd423a6b" :step/content "question-c2c62744-4e6e-5285-9246-bd3c3e1b0773" :step/next "step-f184aec9-5e96-5e74-9416-ea4e8754a3ed" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f184aec9-5e96-5e74-9416-ea4e8754a3ed" :step/id #uuid "f184aec9-5e96-5e74-9416-ea4e8754a3ed" :step/content "question-22229a6c-eb9f-551d-9867-ebd127134155" :step/next "step-847fa0d2-e5ef-5701-9540-0f72c70df40e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-847fa0d2-e5ef-5701-9540-0f72c70df40e" :step/id #uuid "847fa0d2-e5ef-5701-9540-0f72c70df40e" :step/content "question-1bf2ec1b-375e-51fb-ba57-6d1d010ff509" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186066223 :answer-field/choices 17592186066224] [:db/retract 17592186066223 :answer-field/choices 17592186066226] [:db/retract 17592186066223 :answer-field/choices 17592186066227] [:db/retract 17592186066223 :answer-field/choices 17592186066228] {:db/id 17592186069165 :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the minimum point of the function $y=(x-1)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "In general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^{2}$ looks like the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^{2}$ is located at $(h,0).$\n\nFor the given function $y=(x-1)^{2},$ we have $h=1.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ after being translated $1$ unit *right*. \n\n![](images/90/9038cd5affa57a94334ab4af4d8a41bd1702f8f2664c0ae5b30ff9e74b6bfe90.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(1,0),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066223 :answer-field/correct "answer-99afc4ff-5081-5382-8d76-a429936c66c1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5b55fd87-be25-53e2-b4ea-5f79b56d3f15" "answer-10433957-955f-515d-b219-a3e5e64ab10a" "answer-e9788314-4bac-5767-9414-934b60b27a81" "answer-99afc4ff-5081-5382-8d76-a429936c66c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b55fd87-be25-53e2-b4ea-5f79b56d3f15" :answer/id #uuid "5b55fd87-be25-53e2-b4ea-5f79b56d3f15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10433957-955f-515d-b219-a3e5e64ab10a" :answer/id #uuid "10433957-955f-515d-b219-a3e5e64ab10a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9788314-4bac-5767-9414-934b60b27a81" :answer/id #uuid "e9788314-4bac-5767-9414-934b60b27a81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99afc4ff-5081-5382-8d76-a429936c66c1" :answer/id #uuid "99afc4ff-5081-5382-8d76-a429936c66c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068865 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-52805580-1159-55ea-b90b-d6e5fe978449"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-52805580-1159-55ea-b90b-d6e5fe978449" :question/id #uuid "52805580-1159-55ea-b90b-d6e5fe978449" :question/math-academy-id "q-44125" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the minimum point of the function $y=(x+5)^{2}+3?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the graph of $y=(x-h)^{2}+k$ is a translation of the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of $y=(x-h)^{2}+k$ is located at $(h,k).$\n\nHere, the parabola is translated $5$ units *left*, so we have $h=-5.$ The parabola is also translated $3$ units *up*, so we have $k=3.$ \n\n![](images/25/25221241ae83ee4bf78c712ea70d8e4cabea2cb569f59be3122412bbb2cae886.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(-5,3),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4503f107-42ae-5b4a-9180-c8d66e223760"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4503f107-42ae-5b4a-9180-c8d66e223760" :answer-field/id #uuid "4503f107-42ae-5b4a-9180-c8d66e223760" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0fbd013f-2293-5e77-8c84-75f43f728035" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3444194b-8678-5a19-ae45-7bbdb47a4449" "answer-4e01fe47-5f9d-5e9e-8d83-1c0184b6b57d" "answer-0fbd013f-2293-5e77-8c84-75f43f728035" "answer-8faeb4a2-e9b2-5b4b-8ad9-10364ef9120a" "answer-5d141526-53c1-5e1d-98c5-1d888938debb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3444194b-8678-5a19-ae45-7bbdb47a4449" :answer/id #uuid "3444194b-8678-5a19-ae45-7bbdb47a4449" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,-5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e01fe47-5f9d-5e9e-8d83-1c0184b6b57d" :answer/id #uuid "4e01fe47-5f9d-5e9e-8d83-1c0184b6b57d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-5,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fbd013f-2293-5e77-8c84-75f43f728035" :answer/id #uuid "0fbd013f-2293-5e77-8c84-75f43f728035" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-5,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8faeb4a2-e9b2-5b4b-8ad9-10364ef9120a" :answer/id #uuid "8faeb4a2-e9b2-5b4b-8ad9-10364ef9120a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d141526-53c1-5e1d-98c5-1d888938debb" :answer/id #uuid "5d141526-53c1-5e1d-98c5-1d888938debb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068913 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-efac71ed-19b4-5792-ad4d-1b9a5a0c61f5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-efac71ed-19b4-5792-ad4d-1b9a5a0c61f5" :question/id #uuid "efac71ed-19b4-5792-ad4d-1b9a5a0c61f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-105993" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={x}^{2}+2?$" :question/worked-solution "In general, the graph of $y={x}^{2}+k$ looks like the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ but translated $k$ units up. The vertex of $y={x}^{2}+k$ is located at $(0,k).$\n\nIn the given function $y={x}^{2}+2,$ we have $k=2.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ after being translated $2$ units *up*. The vertex is located at $(0,2).$\n\n![](images/c6/c6e9f0867c9b5c04ed1390b01ece62d1fb33673e1e17373a92b2b2d576aeae05.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-83e97490-9261-5f62-9721-770ba25c97fd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-83e97490-9261-5f62-9721-770ba25c97fd" :answer-field/id #uuid "83e97490-9261-5f62-9721-770ba25c97fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-97edee4a-01af-5d30-871f-ce2a7eaf7c0c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-99d2292b-7969-5f6e-b4be-489782f9c227" "answer-628b4d1f-e37b-58c0-8900-4e0915047ca9" "answer-f857a790-7014-5955-9907-0170f0550108" "answer-f3fa6b7b-ab5b-56c3-962c-b8150a7fa208" "answer-97edee4a-01af-5d30-871f-ce2a7eaf7c0c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99d2292b-7969-5f6e-b4be-489782f9c227" :answer/id #uuid "99d2292b-7969-5f6e-b4be-489782f9c227" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/23/23f6df1a9903286f7159a50fe9ff5839b3a99394d54f5f263f0d7850b6ff92b6.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-628b4d1f-e37b-58c0-8900-4e0915047ca9" :answer/id #uuid "628b4d1f-e37b-58c0-8900-4e0915047ca9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/34/3498d6d4b1d62c7c45b397335ebc7159c8e4eb8546bc368a558f30bdc5301847.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f857a790-7014-5955-9907-0170f0550108" :answer/id #uuid "f857a790-7014-5955-9907-0170f0550108" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b0/b0d3e1033e38655f6dbe32901c1016520ba6bc0de566c85176ec74530066966f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3fa6b7b-ab5b-56c3-962c-b8150a7fa208" :answer/id #uuid "f3fa6b7b-ab5b-56c3-962c-b8150a7fa208" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c0/c0c34b26cc2bf965517f37f3ab1e64a55b93b1313d7de9a2fe41b3157fd68dd2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97edee4a-01af-5d30-871f-ce2a7eaf7c0c" :answer/id #uuid "97edee4a-01af-5d30-871f-ce2a7eaf7c0c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c6/c6e9f0867c9b5c04ed1390b01ece62d1fb33673e1e17373a92b2b2d576aeae05.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a503263b-892b-55b8-b8f0-5e26b9407309" :activity/id #uuid "a503263b-892b-55b8-b8f0-5e26b9407309" :activity/title "Graphing Elementary Quadratic Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049620 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-9a92d698-ec86-5778-ba91-fdbd7435c43c" "step-3eba1d14-5e59-5593-ae5f-1e6a34da31f5" "step-625a33d7-26f2-57a4-b96c-ea3ad102fd72"] :activity/first-step "step-9a92d698-ec86-5778-ba91-fdbd7435c43c" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9a92d698-ec86-5778-ba91-fdbd7435c43c" :step/id #uuid "9a92d698-ec86-5778-ba91-fdbd7435c43c" :step/content 17592186069165 :step/next "step-3eba1d14-5e59-5593-ae5f-1e6a34da31f5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3eba1d14-5e59-5593-ae5f-1e6a34da31f5" :step/id #uuid "3eba1d14-5e59-5593-ae5f-1e6a34da31f5" :step/content "question-52805580-1159-55ea-b90b-d6e5fe978449" :step/next "step-625a33d7-26f2-57a4-b96c-ea3ad102fd72" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-625a33d7-26f2-57a4-b96c-ea3ad102fd72" :step/id #uuid "625a33d7-26f2-57a4-b96c-ea3ad102fd72" :step/content "question-efac71ed-19b4-5792-ad4d-1b9a5a0c61f5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b3908e50-0fb9-59ae-a2c0-f103ab5a2672"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b3908e50-0fb9-59ae-a2c0-f103ab5a2672" :question/id #uuid "b3908e50-0fb9-59ae-a2c0-f103ab5a2672" :question/math-academy-id "q-335101" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x(t))+{y}^{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2})=\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)=2xy,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0)(0) & {0}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 0-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-57868f10-a5b3-512a-bff8-00ed06ebf96c" "answer-field-2727dd51-3eb4-5fb4-8350-76aec2222f9b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-57868f10-a5b3-512a-bff8-00ed06ebf96c" :answer-field/id #uuid "57868f10-a5b3-512a-bff8-00ed06ebf96c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-42f7c6ee-f47a-5fc3-ac95-1b525faf0dbb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d3a1c24f-85d1-50cc-ba20-7e9e3c409a64" "answer-39a8fd85-2b96-55d0-bc31-e5f8d765cc6e" "answer-75841e3d-0de0-5bf2-8746-ead9d2a1e32d" "answer-42f7c6ee-f47a-5fc3-ac95-1b525faf0dbb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3a1c24f-85d1-50cc-ba20-7e9e3c409a64" :answer/id #uuid "d3a1c24f-85d1-50cc-ba20-7e9e3c409a64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39a8fd85-2b96-55d0-bc31-e5f8d765cc6e" :answer/id #uuid "39a8fd85-2b96-55d0-bc31-e5f8d765cc6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75841e3d-0de0-5bf2-8746-ead9d2a1e32d" :answer/id #uuid "75841e3d-0de0-5bf2-8746-ead9d2a1e32d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42f7c6ee-f47a-5fc3-ac95-1b525faf0dbb" :answer/id #uuid "42f7c6ee-f47a-5fc3-ac95-1b525faf0dbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2727dd51-3eb4-5fb4-8350-76aec2222f9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "2727dd51-3eb4-5fb4-8350-76aec2222f9b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-acc02c1c-f2c6-5e1e-bf80-8fff7b4093f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1c2943ed-5669-530b-9a3f-caa02cfbde60" "answer-acc02c1c-f2c6-5e1e-bf80-8fff7b4093f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c2943ed-5669-530b-9a3f-caa02cfbde60" :answer/id #uuid "1c2943ed-5669-530b-9a3f-caa02cfbde60" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "almost linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acc02c1c-f2c6-5e1e-bf80-8fff7b4093f3" :answer/id #uuid "acc02c1c-f2c6-5e1e-bf80-8fff7b4093f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221418 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7b1e877f-fd37-5577-a12f-56b4c019e4b2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7b1e877f-fd37-5577-a12f-56b4c019e4b2" :question/id #uuid "7b1e877f-fd37-5577-a12f-56b4c019e4b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-336907" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-2x(t)-3 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4y-2x-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4y-2x-3)=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4y-2x-3)=4 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3)=-2y+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & 4 \\\\ 2x & -2y+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1)-2 & 4 \\\\ 2(1) & -2(1)+3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc4e3ec0-7449-5311-a9a7-e21685e5a48c" "answer-field-2eeedb10-572f-5dd3-bedc-0b1891a82abe" "answer-field-e491bcb7-c400-552a-bcdc-d27ce3349eb6" "answer-field-4d5c020c-d812-5830-bb16-2fde66145db3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc4e3ec0-7449-5311-a9a7-e21685e5a48c" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc4e3ec0-7449-5311-a9a7-e21685e5a48c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-963a67aa-962f-5d59-9155-0724e9cbe8fe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-963a67aa-962f-5d59-9155-0724e9cbe8fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-963a67aa-962f-5d59-9155-0724e9cbe8fe" :answer/id #uuid "963a67aa-962f-5d59-9155-0724e9cbe8fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2eeedb10-572f-5dd3-bedc-0b1891a82abe" :answer-field/id #uuid "2eeedb10-572f-5dd3-bedc-0b1891a82abe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3a7bc9df-4eca-55e0-9b4f-1a80b2401b43" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3a7bc9df-4eca-55e0-9b4f-1a80b2401b43"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a7bc9df-4eca-55e0-9b4f-1a80b2401b43" :answer/id #uuid "3a7bc9df-4eca-55e0-9b4f-1a80b2401b43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e491bcb7-c400-552a-bcdc-d27ce3349eb6" :answer-field/id #uuid "e491bcb7-c400-552a-bcdc-d27ce3349eb6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-456d796f-9080-5d24-aff1-a5093a7534f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-456d796f-9080-5d24-aff1-a5093a7534f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-456d796f-9080-5d24-aff1-a5093a7534f3" :answer/id #uuid "456d796f-9080-5d24-aff1-a5093a7534f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4d5c020c-d812-5830-bb16-2fde66145db3" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d5c020c-d812-5830-bb16-2fde66145db3" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-27b788f6-009e-557e-8638-cb1bada0e9ce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27b788f6-009e-557e-8638-cb1bada0e9ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27b788f6-009e-557e-8638-cb1bada0e9ce" :answer/id #uuid "27b788f6-009e-557e-8638-cb1bada0e9ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221419 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f52e822f-6421-5efd-a62a-efb0d9241bfe"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f52e822f-6421-5efd-a62a-efb0d9241bfe" :question/id #uuid "f52e822f-6421-5efd-a62a-efb0d9241bfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-336950" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}+xy-6 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-{y}^{2}+2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}={[2,\\text{ }1]}^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy-6 \\\\ -{y}^{2}+2x-3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy-6)=2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy-6)=x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+2x-3)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+2x-3)=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y & x \\\\ 2 & -2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(2)+1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{2}.$\n\nSince the eigenvalues are real and have opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9d37d7a9-8471-50da-b073-2955e9e0599c" "answer-field-a6eb5f28-ac4d-5b95-b64b-baa3dc1708f6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9d37d7a9-8471-50da-b073-2955e9e0599c" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d37d7a9-8471-50da-b073-2955e9e0599c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-7cd8a3e1-68b3-519c-853a-2545ec83e1d6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-55acf14a-7200-5cda-93be-9d5945787124" "answer-7cd8a3e1-68b3-519c-853a-2545ec83e1d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55acf14a-7200-5cda-93be-9d5945787124" :answer/id #uuid "55acf14a-7200-5cda-93be-9d5945787124" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cd8a3e1-68b3-519c-853a-2545ec83e1d6" :answer/id #uuid "7cd8a3e1-68b3-519c-853a-2545ec83e1d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a6eb5f28-ac4d-5b95-b64b-baa3dc1708f6" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6eb5f28-ac4d-5b95-b64b-baa3dc1708f6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-228bfc62-fb62-5294-a07c-a1a29f6ba804" :answer-field/choices ["answer-228bfc62-fb62-5294-a07c-a1a29f6ba804" "answer-4ae6b8de-662b-54bb-a0ce-647e75cdc4c9" "answer-e1a1e0d7-eefa-5285-8c61-39a8788320e8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-228bfc62-fb62-5294-a07c-a1a29f6ba804" :answer/id #uuid "228bfc62-fb62-5294-a07c-a1a29f6ba804" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ae6b8de-662b-54bb-a0ce-647e75cdc4c9" :answer/id #uuid "4ae6b8de-662b-54bb-a0ce-647e75cdc4c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1a1e0d7-eefa-5285-8c61-39a8788320e8" :answer/id #uuid "e1a1e0d7-eefa-5285-8c61-39a8788320e8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b4984445-7c47-5b70-871c-b5c460821b24" :activity/id #uuid "b4984445-7c47-5b70-871c-b5c460821b24" :activity/title "Linear Approximations Near Equilibria" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048881 :activity/course 17592186046669 :activity/steps ["step-c347bf5d-5480-57e0-a437-6e24c7d9755b" "step-501033d8-e454-5811-b966-77d481fbff5d" "step-593a3c9e-9d69-56ea-ae20-d8b446ff32c3" "step-987ac928-c043-5745-b107-d0a7b5b8d27a" "step-b1d288d6-ba48-510c-9a5c-e09c1e217944"] :activity/first-step "step-c347bf5d-5480-57e0-a437-6e24c7d9755b" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-c347bf5d-5480-57e0-a437-6e24c7d9755b" :step/id #uuid "c347bf5d-5480-57e0-a437-6e24c7d9755b" :step/content 17592186221971 :step/next "step-501033d8-e454-5811-b966-77d481fbff5d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-501033d8-e454-5811-b966-77d481fbff5d" :step/id #uuid "501033d8-e454-5811-b966-77d481fbff5d" :step/content "question-b3908e50-0fb9-59ae-a2c0-f103ab5a2672" :step/next "step-593a3c9e-9d69-56ea-ae20-d8b446ff32c3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-593a3c9e-9d69-56ea-ae20-d8b446ff32c3" :step/id #uuid "593a3c9e-9d69-56ea-ae20-d8b446ff32c3" :step/content 17592186368134 :step/next "step-987ac928-c043-5745-b107-d0a7b5b8d27a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-987ac928-c043-5745-b107-d0a7b5b8d27a" :step/id #uuid "987ac928-c043-5745-b107-d0a7b5b8d27a" :step/content "question-7b1e877f-fd37-5577-a12f-56b4c019e4b2" :step/next "step-b1d288d6-ba48-510c-9a5c-e09c1e217944" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b1d288d6-ba48-510c-9a5c-e09c1e217944" :step/id #uuid "b1d288d6-ba48-510c-9a5c-e09c1e217944" :step/content "question-f52e822f-6421-5efd-a62a-efb0d9241bfe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078559 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b49e8f9b-353c-5ece-9748-47abc8dc5438"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b49e8f9b-353c-5ece-9748-47abc8dc5438" :question/id #uuid "b49e8f9b-353c-5ece-9748-47abc8dc5438" :question/math-academy-id "q-5437" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n1,\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{9}{4},\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=1$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2},$ so we can find the common ratio, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{1}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\frac{3}{2},$  we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-27573e10-1873-5b44-a68a-7f1ad53ececa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-27573e10-1873-5b44-a68a-7f1ad53ececa" :answer-field/id #uuid "27573e10-1873-5b44-a68a-7f1ad53ececa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b595f1c4-cac6-5c27-9491-cf38def6d400" :answer-field/choices ["answer-73e92523-fc3e-578c-9d40-6df5fc8e63ae" "answer-70077891-86db-502d-895f-ded2194be2fc" "answer-72e91449-71dc-56a0-be51-67be87f1ed56" "answer-b3782bf8-ceaf-5b27-82d4-96d98b8fc40e" "answer-b595f1c4-cac6-5c27-9491-cf38def6d400"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73e92523-fc3e-578c-9d40-6df5fc8e63ae" :answer/id #uuid "73e92523-fc3e-578c-9d40-6df5fc8e63ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}=1+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70077891-86db-502d-895f-ded2194be2fc" :answer/id #uuid "70077891-86db-502d-895f-ded2194be2fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72e91449-71dc-56a0-be51-67be87f1ed56" :answer/id #uuid "72e91449-71dc-56a0-be51-67be87f1ed56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3782bf8-ceaf-5b27-82d4-96d98b8fc40e" :answer/id #uuid "b3782bf8-ceaf-5b27-82d4-96d98b8fc40e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b595f1c4-cac6-5c27-9491-cf38def6d400" :answer/id #uuid "b595f1c4-cac6-5c27-9491-cf38def6d400" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078797 :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of a sequence if the $4$th term is $-135$ and the common ratio is $-3.$\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=-3,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $4$th term is ${a}_{4}=-135,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{4-1} \\\\ -135 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{3} \\\\ -135 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-27) \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=5,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5(-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186075730 :answer-field/correct "answer-dced77a5-fd44-5940-8816-bb90dfa191d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dced77a5-fd44-5940-8816-bb90dfa191d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dced77a5-fd44-5940-8816-bb90dfa191d4" :answer/id #uuid "dced77a5-fd44-5940-8816-bb90dfa191d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5(-3)^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078799 :question/problem "If the fifth term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{8}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{4},$ then the eighth term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{4},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{8},$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{5-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{256}) \\\\ \\frac{256}{8} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 32 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=32,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the $8$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{8-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{7} \\\\  & ={2}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{({2}^{2})^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{2}^{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is ${a}_{8}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186075732 :answer-field/correct "answer-83f24e26-b71d-5aeb-8bcf-af942140f378" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83f24e26-b71d-5aeb-8bcf-af942140f378"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83f24e26-b71d-5aeb-8bcf-af942140f378" :answer/id #uuid "83f24e26-b71d-5aeb-8bcf-af942140f378" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{512}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-387c0af8-04ff-5b91-acbd-ef00561b520e" :activity/id #uuid "387c0af8-04ff-5b91-acbd-ef00561b520e" :activity/title "The Nth Term of a Geometric Sequence" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050067 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-b569b644-bc51-538e-91cf-d1aafe61dc67" "step-2e56d537-0b08-5372-bec2-2b132cbebf16" "step-17f20667-bb76-5a5f-90f5-ccb22eb38b24"] :activity/first-step "step-b569b644-bc51-538e-91cf-d1aafe61dc67" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-b569b644-bc51-538e-91cf-d1aafe61dc67" :step/id #uuid "b569b644-bc51-538e-91cf-d1aafe61dc67" :step/content "question-b49e8f9b-353c-5ece-9748-47abc8dc5438" :step/next "step-2e56d537-0b08-5372-bec2-2b132cbebf16" :db/ensur