cdot (-5)+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4+10+2+0 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186118352 :answer-field/correct 17592186118353 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-99f979f5-50b6-5282-8195-712172f0dd94" :question/id #uuid "99f979f5-50b6-5282-8195-712172f0dd94" :question/math-academy-id "q-105106" :question/problem "Let $r=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ What is $r\\cdot s?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vectors:\n\n- The vector $r=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has $4$ components as it lies in ${R}^{4}.$\n\n- The vector $s=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has $5$ components as it lies in ${R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, since the number of components does not match, the operation is undefined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0fb98a7f-ad8a-5e6c-a75e-a840dc8becf9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0fb98a7f-ad8a-5e6c-a75e-a840dc8becf9" :answer-field/id #uuid "0fb98a7f-ad8a-5e6c-a75e-a840dc8becf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-16cbb84b-4b49-534a-9632-2742c64dc828" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e450beec-fb91-51ce-9266-c0780d069541" "answer-3d40e247-e7a6-52d9-b061-d0eb12e43950" "answer-99bc17e3-08dd-536d-b086-370a117aaf2b" "answer-7449c50e-1ec7-5e2c-ab02-d370a373173c" "answer-16cbb84b-4b49-534a-9632-2742c64dc828"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e450beec-fb91-51ce-9266-c0780d069541" :answer/id #uuid "e450beec-fb91-51ce-9266-c0780d069541" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d40e247-e7a6-52d9-b061-d0eb12e43950" :answer/id #uuid "3d40e247-e7a6-52d9-b061-d0eb12e43950" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99bc17e3-08dd-536d-b086-370a117aaf2b" :answer/id #uuid "99bc17e3-08dd-536d-b086-370a117aaf2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7449c50e-1ec7-5e2c-ab02-d370a373173c" :answer/id #uuid "7449c50e-1ec7-5e2c-ab02-d370a373173c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16cbb84b-4b49-534a-9632-2742c64dc828" :answer/id #uuid "16cbb84b-4b49-534a-9632-2742c64dc828" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-36b464cf-4c70-5aa3-a6c7-ba1498951d9c" :question/id #uuid "36b464cf-4c70-5aa3-a6c7-ba1498951d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73943" :question/problem "Let\n$r=[\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ x \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $r\\cdot s=7$, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the equation $r\\cdot s=7,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot s & =7 \\\\ (x-2)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot x+1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1 & =7 \\\\ x-2+6-3x-2+1 & =7 \\\\ -2x+3 & =7 \\\\ 2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4b8ec564-aa1b-5852-8d2e-5e288d532861"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4b8ec564-aa1b-5852-8d2e-5e288d532861" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b8ec564-aa1b-5852-8d2e-5e288d532861" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-41ca8a64-2cf5-52f3-9711-01557d3f9064" :answer-field/choices ["answer-41ca8a64-2cf5-52f3-9711-01557d3f9064" "answer-5e6868a9-5b3e-5e84-a960-5b86e70a2ee6" "answer-1b14a075-42dc-5acb-b0ba-f7abb08019c2" "answer-3e03f259-c4ff-5558-b956-a6de172b8d43" "answer-681716cd-d4da-5905-8de6-fe282cc9d42b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41ca8a64-2cf5-52f3-9711-01557d3f9064" :answer/id #uuid "41ca8a64-2cf5-52f3-9711-01557d3f9064" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e6868a9-5b3e-5e84-a960-5b86e70a2ee6" :answer/id #uuid "5e6868a9-5b3e-5e84-a960-5b86e70a2ee6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b14a075-42dc-5acb-b0ba-f7abb08019c2" :answer/id #uuid "1b14a075-42dc-5acb-b0ba-f7abb08019c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e03f259-c4ff-5558-b956-a6de172b8d43" :answer/id #uuid "3e03f259-c4ff-5558-b956-a6de172b8d43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-681716cd-d4da-5905-8de6-fe282cc9d42b" :answer/id #uuid "681716cd-d4da-5905-8de6-fe282cc9d42b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ea418b13-9cd8-58b9-8c0f-d233456f224d" :question/id #uuid "ea418b13-9cd8-58b9-8c0f-d233456f224d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73944" :question/problem "Let\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ p \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ p\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $v\\cdot w=3$, find the value of $p.$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the equation $v\\cdot w=3,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $p$ and then solve for $p$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot w & =3 \\\\ -2\\cdot 2+p\\cdot (-4)+5\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot p & =3 \\\\ -4-4p+15+6-3p & =3 \\\\ -7p+17 & =3 \\\\ 7p & =14 \\\\ p & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $p=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3e1fad6f-855e-553e-8d75-e321cc680343"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3e1fad6f-855e-553e-8d75-e321cc680343" :answer-field/id #uuid "3e1fad6f-855e-553e-8d75-e321cc680343" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d0c098d1-7ef1-5ad1-b259-c1614202d6d3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b5008f77-48d1-5bb2-ba5f-fe9365aea196" "answer-d0c098d1-7ef1-5ad1-b259-c1614202d6d3" "answer-d290f871-27b8-5194-87df-869b3c3e63ff" "answer-444efbe8-dff8-5c45-a4c6-ad837d7e5d57" "answer-87e9f515-7eb3-5e62-a8a0-a8403b398027"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5008f77-48d1-5bb2-ba5f-fe9365aea196" :answer/id #uuid "b5008f77-48d1-5bb2-ba5f-fe9365aea196" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0c098d1-7ef1-5ad1-b259-c1614202d6d3" :answer/id #uuid "d0c098d1-7ef1-5ad1-b259-c1614202d6d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d290f871-27b8-5194-87df-869b3c3e63ff" :answer/id #uuid "d290f871-27b8-5194-87df-869b3c3e63ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-444efbe8-dff8-5c45-a4c6-ad837d7e5d57" :answer/id #uuid "444efbe8-dff8-5c45-a4c6-ad837d7e5d57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87e9f515-7eb3-5e62-a8a0-a8403b398027" :answer/id #uuid "87e9f515-7eb3-5e62-a8a0-a8403b398027" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-99569175-b9f3-5c01-983c-724e408918e3" :question/id #uuid "99569175-b9f3-5c01-983c-724e408918e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-73947" :question/problem "Let $r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ {x}^{4} \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $r\\cdot s=0$ and $x<0$, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the equation $r\\cdot s=0,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot s & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot {x}^{4}+0\\cdot 8+x\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ -3+2{x}^{2}+0-x & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-x-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-1)\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4(2)(-3)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{25}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get two solutions, ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=-1.$ Since we need $x<0$, our final answer is $x=-1$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-17f4d7b6-1d54-58dd-a546-544bac478642"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-17f4d7b6-1d54-58dd-a546-544bac478642" :answer-field/id #uuid "17f4d7b6-1d54-58dd-a546-544bac478642" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-548a5160-ea2a-5460-be28-4daa3a49d1cb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-548a5160-ea2a-5460-be28-4daa3a49d1cb" "answer-a5314088-f525-53ae-8f0e-c1cb3cc6d421" "answer-b29fdb02-3b26-5216-8f4e-13b529bbcd39" "answer-4a8a54af-e3aa-5606-a52d-b582cbbc8cca" "answer-280517df-9242-59ab-bc3e-65af0d2d37ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-548a5160-ea2a-5460-be28-4daa3a49d1cb" :answer/id #uuid "548a5160-ea2a-5460-be28-4daa3a49d1cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5314088-f525-53ae-8f0e-c1cb3cc6d421" :answer/id #uuid "a5314088-f525-53ae-8f0e-c1cb3cc6d421" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b29fdb02-3b26-5216-8f4e-13b529bbcd39" :answer/id #uuid "b29fdb02-3b26-5216-8f4e-13b529bbcd39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a8a54af-e3aa-5606-a52d-b582cbbc8cca" :answer/id #uuid "4a8a54af-e3aa-5606-a52d-b582cbbc8cca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-280517df-9242-59ab-bc3e-65af0d2d37ee" :answer/id #uuid "280517df-9242-59ab-bc3e-65af0d2d37ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac43b1b5-6764-5241-bdc0-c6b7a39ba60f" :question/id #uuid "ac43b1b5-6764-5241-bdc0-c6b7a39ba60f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35098" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k+2\\end{aligned}].$ If $u\\cdot v=0$, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the equation $u\\cdot v=0,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $k$ and then solve for $k$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot k+3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-5)+6\\cdot (k+2) & =0 \\\\ k+12-10+6k+12 & =0 \\\\ 7k+14 & =0 \\\\ 7k & =-14 \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $k=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-513e934d-104b-54ec-9e02-0f0638cf1a17"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-513e934d-104b-54ec-9e02-0f0638cf1a17" :answer-field/id #uuid "513e934d-104b-54ec-9e02-0f0638cf1a17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3c75b571-33fa-5088-b2e0-5d3674cd21af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c75b571-33fa-5088-b2e0-5d3674cd21af" "answer-7337f43d-3302-55c7-837c-201949c779c0" "answer-14c7e230-fd06-52b6-a9bf-f5d1185d3530" "answer-ab52cab0-c0f9-5d04-a911-88760c863129" "answer-d6c70620-90f9-5604-afb9-68b09a4d75d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c75b571-33fa-5088-b2e0-5d3674cd21af" :answer/id #uuid "3c75b571-33fa-5088-b2e0-5d3674cd21af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7337f43d-3302-55c7-837c-201949c779c0" :answer/id #uuid "7337f43d-3302-55c7-837c-201949c779c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14c7e230-fd06-52b6-a9bf-f5d1185d3530" :answer/id #uuid "14c7e230-fd06-52b6-a9bf-f5d1185d3530" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab52cab0-c0f9-5d04-a911-88760c863129" :answer/id #uuid "ab52cab0-c0f9-5d04-a911-88760c863129" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6c70620-90f9-5604-afb9-68b09a4d75d9" :answer/id #uuid "d6c70620-90f9-5604-afb9-68b09a4d75d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{24}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121062 :tutorial/content "To add two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we just need to add their corresponding components. To illustrate, consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4,$ given by\n\n![](images/1f/1f1b79f2e61fccbb27944284cbdd217a311a7f795100cfbb56340a457814b407.svg)\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b},$ all we have to do is to add each component of the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ with the corresponding component of $\\mathbf{b}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5+(-5) \\\\ 1+3 \\\\ -3+1 \\\\ 6+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can only compute the sum, difference, or dot product of two vectors if both vectors have the same number of components. For example, we can't find the following sum, so we say it's *undefined*:\n![](images/6c/6c377966aa9fa1d581c0f9e3ba16bceb4020471914d5c4fb61118ff7cd709466.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121033 :tutorial/content "In general, if we have two vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ and a scalar $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R},$ then our vector operations are defined as follows:\n\n1\\. The **sum/difference** of the two vectors is defined as\n  ![](images/fc/fc78b2f4e9262108a217e57fec67e096b8b8b18e1486bec8e51df60b8afdfdfa.svg)\n\n2\\. **Scalar multiplication** of a vector is defined as\n  ![](images/e7/e78025f3481423365b2e924b6f8ba4e59f41e0c644ced7d2263238b219cba27a.svg)\n\n3\\. The **dot product** of two vectors is defined as\n\n  $$\n  \\quad \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = \\underbrace{u_1 \\cdot v_1 + u_2 \\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n \\cdot v_n}_{\\Large{n} \\,\\textrm{-terms}} = \\sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i.\n  $$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083390 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b91a0507-397b-5914-a663-1432f07abc1f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082919 :question/worked-solution "Let us call $A$ the event \"The patient is a smoker\" and $B$ the event \"The patient has diabetes.\" \n\nSince none of the diabetic patients are smokers, we draw two non-overlapping circles to represent the events $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Since there are $10$ smokers, we write the number $10$ within the circle $A.$\n\n- Since there are $12$ patients with diabetes, we write the number $12$ within the circle $B.$\n\nThere are $25$ patients in total, so the number of remaining patients is \n\n$$\n25 - 10 - 12 = 3.\n$$\n\nTo represent the $3$ remaining patients, we write the number $3$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nFinally, our Venn diagram looks as follows:\n\n![](images/86/86855ecd3f1199c9a5c6fbb66ccf08d5b8dd2913d358f52d2c499039ea6fda4d.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083391 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0b138f98-60f4-5926-8616-f98d6e7b3754" "question-c12c5e9b-72e8-5854-9bf8-a8cf7ba5e8c9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082920 :question/problem "The Venn diagram shown below represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes on each region of the sample space. Given that every outcome is equally likely, find $P(A\\cup B).$\n\n![](images/34/34ab66b54fcf6be9740be1fc89bd436ccc8d334dc8b20a5769993a93bd05b2f9.png)" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A\\cup B$ is represented by the area that is in either circle (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/b2/b25a0b74f7b5242f783c38c81bdb4bf8025a847f7079fc90febe510fec041b6d.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A \\cup B$ is\n\n$$\n20+15+25=60.\n$$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n20+15+25+40=100.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\dfrac{60}{100}= 0.6.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083392 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cc44bf24-a689-5cdc-ad9e-c41be1cff05a" "question-9ac83742-fbb3-5922-b2ad-70bce502b290"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082925 :question/worked-solution "Let us call $A$ the event \"The student plays piano\" and $B$ the event \"The student plays violin.\"\n\nNote that there are $22$ students who play both piano and violin. We draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $22$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who play *only* the piano is\n\n$$\n68 - 22 = 46,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $46$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of students who play *only* the violin is\n\n$$\n39 - 22 = 17,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $17$ in the part of the circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A.$\n\nThe total number of students who play at least one of the instruments is\n\n$$\n22+46+17 = 85,\n$$\n\nand there are $100$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is\n\n$$\n100 - 85 = 15.\n$$\n\nTo represent these $15$ students who play neither piano nor violin, we write the number $15$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nTherefore, our Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](images/cc/cc61c2a5c61467ac19d5debe2c2e53dc48f06f8d153c014f4a73e6feed514900.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to students who play at least one of the instruments. The probability that a randomly selected student is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP\\left(A\\cup B\\right)=\\dfrac{85}{100}= 0.85.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083698 :question/worked-solution "Note that there are $10$ students who study both languages. So, we draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $10$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who *only* study English is\n\n$$\n43-10=33,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $33$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of students who *only* study Spanish is\n\n$$\n25-10=15,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $15$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A.$\n\nThere are $63$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is \n\n$$\n63-10-33-15=5.\n$$\n\nTo represent the $5$ remaining students, we write the number $5$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nFinally, our Venn diagram looks as follows:\n\n![](images/3d/3da8b95c5182be1fd4c027e5e6f2ce84274286c805afdc84891bd5aecd14add9.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186081119 :answer-field/correct 17592186081122 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-b91a0507-397b-5914-a663-1432f07abc1f" :question/id #uuid "b91a0507-397b-5914-a663-1432f07abc1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-121179" :question/problem "In a school, a survey conducted on the $40$ students shows that $17$ students traveled to school by train, $23$ students traveled by bus, and $10$ students traveled by both train and bus. \n\nIf $T$ represents the event that a student travels by train, and $B$ represents the event that a student travels by bus, which of the following diagrams gives the correct Venn diagram for the corresponding sample space?" :question/worked-solution "Note that there are $10$ students who traveled by both train and bus. We draw two overlapping circles representing the events $T$ and $B$ and write the number $10$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who traveled by train *only*  is\n\n$$\n17-10=7,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $7$ in the part of circle $T$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of students who traveled by bus *only*  is\n\n$$\n23-10=13,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $13$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $T.$\n\nThere are $40$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is \n\n$$\n40-7-10-13=10.\n$$\n\nTo represent the $10$ remaining students, we write the number $10$ outside of the circles $T$ and $B.$\n\nFinally, our Venn diagram looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a5/a511835f1ecf6a7f3991f8ebb9ed14339ebfab4cb41aa06363502df5abf03719.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a0501cb1-59d9-5e7f-87d0-8d8554465408"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a0501cb1-59d9-5e7f-87d0-8d8554465408" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0501cb1-59d9-5e7f-87d0-8d8554465408" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2a1bdf2a-96e9-51d9-a76e-47feb46900c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b1f0de5-0f2b-5045-865e-ad8049a87a40" "answer-f5a514d8-34b0-5fdf-afd1-074936c6ce73" "answer-2d59aa09-be93-5eee-8d36-aa7f2a6de334" "answer-ab239710-f6f9-5fa9-9991-5174c45175ed" "answer-2a1bdf2a-96e9-51d9-a76e-47feb46900c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b1f0de5-0f2b-5045-865e-ad8049a87a40" :answer/id #uuid "8b1f0de5-0f2b-5045-865e-ad8049a87a40" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/06/069844b0c1506a869fbefe3a0646416a5fa24b2eb9deecd819b0ff3bf60d55e2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5a514d8-34b0-5fdf-afd1-074936c6ce73" :answer/id #uuid "f5a514d8-34b0-5fdf-afd1-074936c6ce73" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4c/4cd74e5a929964ad92259971a2b0cf83201e4d1ff912cb5d5d0880da1006a222.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d59aa09-be93-5eee-8d36-aa7f2a6de334" :answer/id #uuid "2d59aa09-be93-5eee-8d36-aa7f2a6de334" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/65/652f1e12bca4eaf43e7ad8d8c9de0a77f9d3f9904d84e10cd86a366ff7f4abaa.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab239710-f6f9-5fa9-9991-5174c45175ed" :answer/id #uuid "ab239710-f6f9-5fa9-9991-5174c45175ed" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/7d/7d79399dfd4e3a0eedcccbfc878999e67c6998b4d500bdb30fecb8f9a805fd57.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a1bdf2a-96e9-51d9-a76e-47feb46900c8" :answer/id #uuid "2a1bdf2a-96e9-51d9-a76e-47feb46900c8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a5/a511835f1ecf6a7f3991f8ebb9ed14339ebfab4cb41aa06363502df5abf03719.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0b138f98-60f4-5926-8616-f98d6e7b3754" :question/id #uuid "0b138f98-60f4-5926-8616-f98d6e7b3754" :question/math-academy-id "q-11627" :question/problem "![](images/20/20aaaae06d88f9105276f77bb65975e7d775c8fd15fbf4984d9b1889e73503dd.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space. Given that every outcome is equally likely, find $P(A\\cap B)$ to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A\\cap B$ is represented by the area that is inside the overlap between the circles (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/c0/c0c7df273c9b71fc0286b03d7405cb8ca473d14b1020fc1ec15530879ddb8f1c.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A\\cap B$ is $15.$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n20+15+25+40=100.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B)=\\frac{15}{100}=\\frac{3}{20}=0.15\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-332525cc-a511-5267-bb34-aaa17e24cd11"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-332525cc-a511-5267-bb34-aaa17e24cd11" :answer-field/id #uuid "332525cc-a511-5267-bb34-aaa17e24cd11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-09cc727b-a898-5540-97d6-f2c9b147974f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-09cc727b-a898-5540-97d6-f2c9b147974f" "answer-e3d2cbdf-7de0-5fe4-813a-7b2573e8fd31" "answer-0b0f1776-a062-5fb8-a2c8-2000dc8577c5" "answer-834552c3-6f8a-5fed-ba39-04a42ef90102" "answer-496c5835-4952-55d5-9f5b-9ea6b8e16eab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09cc727b-a898-5540-97d6-f2c9b147974f" :answer/id #uuid "09cc727b-a898-5540-97d6-f2c9b147974f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3d2cbdf-7de0-5fe4-813a-7b2573e8fd31" :answer/id #uuid "e3d2cbdf-7de0-5fe4-813a-7b2573e8fd31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b0f1776-a062-5fb8-a2c8-2000dc8577c5" :answer/id #uuid "0b0f1776-a062-5fb8-a2c8-2000dc8577c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-834552c3-6f8a-5fed-ba39-04a42ef90102" :answer/id #uuid "834552c3-6f8a-5fed-ba39-04a42ef90102" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.37" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-496c5835-4952-55d5-9f5b-9ea6b8e16eab" :answer/id #uuid "496c5835-4952-55d5-9f5b-9ea6b8e16eab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c12c5e9b-72e8-5854-9bf8-a8cf7ba5e8c9" :question/id #uuid "c12c5e9b-72e8-5854-9bf8-a8cf7ba5e8c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-120043" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5f197f52df26da43b27b2b142f25462570828996ea28154f865bac2e871e972.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space. Given that every outcome is equally likely, find $P(A\\cup B)$ to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A\\cup B$ is represented by the area that is inside both circles (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/ad/ad51768e560488687b0dfbd573f71136f7863106d0a830828878bc5488b05daf.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A\\cup B$ is\n\n$$\n6+1+4=11.\n$$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n6+1+4+12=23.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\frac{11}{23}≈0.48\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2259f512-8e22-5ed6-bb49-42aefe325560"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2259f512-8e22-5ed6-bb49-42aefe325560" :answer-field/id #uuid "2259f512-8e22-5ed6-bb49-42aefe325560" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8a94bf5f-4707-5650-aeb1-640b72693ca7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-364cf66d-3412-5141-8d2d-02ccae075c69" "answer-6a78e3e7-3db8-5d5f-bc92-b9b93cb03eb0" "answer-0d507738-9fa2-5896-a470-f0b105f24c04" "answer-8a94bf5f-4707-5650-aeb1-640b72693ca7" "answer-d25a2107-29ae-50bf-96cf-132fc63087e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-364cf66d-3412-5141-8d2d-02ccae075c69" :answer/id #uuid "364cf66d-3412-5141-8d2d-02ccae075c69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.04" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a78e3e7-3db8-5d5f-bc92-b9b93cb03eb0" :answer/id #uuid "6a78e3e7-3db8-5d5f-bc92-b9b93cb03eb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.38" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d507738-9fa2-5896-a470-f0b105f24c04" :answer/id #uuid "0d507738-9fa2-5896-a470-f0b105f24c04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.43" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a94bf5f-4707-5650-aeb1-640b72693ca7" :answer/id #uuid "8a94bf5f-4707-5650-aeb1-640b72693ca7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d25a2107-29ae-50bf-96cf-132fc63087e7" :answer/id #uuid "d25a2107-29ae-50bf-96cf-132fc63087e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cc44bf24-a689-5cdc-ad9e-c41be1cff05a" :question/id #uuid "cc44bf24-a689-5cdc-ad9e-c41be1cff05a" :question/math-academy-id "q-121180" :question/problem "In a class of $30$ students, $18$ have black hair, $9$ have blue eyes and $7$ have both black hair and blue eyes. Draw a Venn diagram representing this sample space. If a student is selected at random among the $30$ students, what is the probability that the student has black hair or blue eyes? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let us call $A$ the event \"the student has black hair\" and $B$ the event \"the student has blue eyes.\"\n\nNote that there are $7$ students who have both black hair and blue eyes. So, we draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $7$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who *only* have black hair is\n\n$$\n18-7=11,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $11$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of students who *only* have blue eyes is\n\n$$\n9-7=2,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $2$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A.$\n\nThere are $30$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is \n\n$$\n30-11-7-2=10.\n$$\n\nTo represent these $10$ students that have not black hair or blue eyes, we write the number $10$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nTherefore, our Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](images/fa/fab4a1a0244dc20c692101b35bac04ca9e73f4458a709d2507ba8d9a21345109.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to students that have black hair or blue eyes. The probability that a randomly selected student is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\frac{11+7+2}{30}=\\frac{20}{30}≈0.67,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-104a8090-a9a9-505d-bae0-c829e3a97494"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-104a8090-a9a9-505d-bae0-c829e3a97494" :answer-field/id #uuid "104a8090-a9a9-505d-bae0-c829e3a97494" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2eba1f03-5431-549e-b34c-44334143652f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-adf10555-c1f1-5075-9060-56b21226e412" "answer-18daee96-9926-51bc-aa9c-54bcce231698" "answer-6833c09e-bc27-5478-af5c-17877146d61c" "answer-e154eea1-3d2f-5893-9370-222beb6bfa7a" "answer-2eba1f03-5431-549e-b34c-44334143652f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adf10555-c1f1-5075-9060-56b21226e412" :answer/id #uuid "adf10555-c1f1-5075-9060-56b21226e412" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18daee96-9926-51bc-aa9c-54bcce231698" :answer/id #uuid "18daee96-9926-51bc-aa9c-54bcce231698" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6833c09e-bc27-5478-af5c-17877146d61c" :answer/id #uuid "6833c09e-bc27-5478-af5c-17877146d61c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.37" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e154eea1-3d2f-5893-9370-222beb6bfa7a" :answer/id #uuid "e154eea1-3d2f-5893-9370-222beb6bfa7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.54" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2eba1f03-5431-549e-b34c-44334143652f" :answer/id #uuid "2eba1f03-5431-549e-b34c-44334143652f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.67" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ac83742-fbb3-5922-b2ad-70bce502b290" :question/id #uuid "9ac83742-fbb3-5922-b2ad-70bce502b290" :question/math-academy-id "q-11631" :question/problem "At a band audition, there are $50$ singers. There are $27$ country singers, $23$ pop singers, and $6$ country and pop singers. Draw a Venn diagram representing this sample space. If a singer is selected at random among the $50$ singers, what is the probability that the singer sings country or pop music (or both)? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let us call $A$ the event \"the singer sings country music\" and $B$ the event \"the singer sings pop music.\"\n\nNote that there are $6$ singers singing both country and pop music. So, we draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $6$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of singers who *only* sing country music is\n\n$$\n27-6=21,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $21$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of singers who *only* sing pop music is\n\n$$\n23-6=17,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $17$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A.$\n\nThe total number of singers who sing country or pop music is\n\n$$\n6+21+17=44,\n$$\n\nand there are $50$ singers in total, so the number of remaining singers is\n\n$$\n50-44=6.\n$$\n\nTo represent these $6$ singers who did not sing country or pop music, we write the number $6$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nTherefore, our Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](images/02/0240b83c3de5c8c3f3b1e91d3b9c8ae2899f0b2a18611d7e70b6dbf71f23e25b.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to singers that sing country or pop music (or both). The probability that a randomly selected singer is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\frac{44}{50}=0.88.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae364575-09e6-5cf9-9c82-8eb16e6ba56e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae364575-09e6-5cf9-9c82-8eb16e6ba56e" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae364575-09e6-5cf9-9c82-8eb16e6ba56e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cb8c28da-829d-5a0f-bd54-32c167c55f41" :answer-field/choices ["answer-23feca0a-70cb-543d-b0d9-51f705b682a7" "answer-97041577-9ffb-5796-9c28-3ac685cc9cb0" "answer-48fdc177-f885-5f30-a54e-78e80947ab4a" "answer-297c5b88-2fb7-580a-8e17-ad860e113bd7" "answer-cb8c28da-829d-5a0f-bd54-32c167c55f41"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23feca0a-70cb-543d-b0d9-51f705b682a7" :answer/id #uuid "23feca0a-70cb-543d-b0d9-51f705b682a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.72" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97041577-9ffb-5796-9c28-3ac685cc9cb0" :answer/id #uuid "97041577-9ffb-5796-9c28-3ac685cc9cb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.76" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48fdc177-f885-5f30-a54e-78e80947ab4a" :answer/id #uuid "48fdc177-f885-5f30-a54e-78e80947ab4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.83" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-297c5b88-2fb7-580a-8e17-ad860e113bd7" :answer/id #uuid "297c5b88-2fb7-580a-8e17-ad860e113bd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.85" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb8c28da-829d-5a0f-bd54-32c167c55f41" :answer/id #uuid "cb8c28da-829d-5a0f-bd54-32c167c55f41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.88" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084508 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a bag of tiles labeled $a,b,c,d,e,$ and $f,$ where the bag contains an equal number of each tile. We draw a single tile at random from the bag. The sample space $\\mathcal S$ of all possible outcomes is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal S = \\{ a, b, c, d, e, f \\}.\n$$\n\nLet's define two events, $A$ and $B,$ as follows:\n\n- Let $A$ be the event that we draw a vowel. Then, we have\n\n$$\nA = \\{ a, e \\}.\n$$\n\n- Let $B$ be the event that we get one of the first three letters of the alphabet. Then, we have\n\n$$\nB = \\{ a, b, c \\}.\n$$\n\nHow do we calculate $P(A\\cap B)?$\n\nLet's use a Venn diagram representation for $A\\cap B,$ as shown below. Notice that we haven't included the probabilities, just the values contained within $\\mathcal S.$\n\n![](images/02/027955ed4e60b71c008c054c29c47bdaf092c4f4aff255327ea3c5bda7788f60.png)\n\nLet's construct another Venn diagram in which we count the number of entries in each region of the Venn diagram above.\n\n![](images/43/43790d39be47480338fdd4df095854de3757b36ce9bc659d60f9b3a6e8688269.png)\n\nWe're told that there is an equal number of each tile, so every outcome in the sample space is equally likely. Therefore, to compute $P(A \\cap B),$ we need to count the number of events in $A \\cap B$ and divide by the total number of events in $\\mathcal S.$\n\n- In a Venn diagram, $A \\cap B$ is represented by the area where the circles overlap. From the diagram above, we see that there is only $\\color{red}1$ event in $A \\cap B.$\n\n- The total number of events in $\\mathcal S$ is $1+1+2+2={\\color{blue}{6}}.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = \\frac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}6}.\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region representing the event we're interested in and divide by the total number of outcomes in the sample space $\\mathcal S.$ \n\nFinally, it is important to remember that this method works *only* if every outcome in the sample space is equally likely." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083471 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162097 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-352fc2e3-10d1-5d70-be93-5831359ed522"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-352fc2e3-10d1-5d70-be93-5831359ed522" :question/id #uuid "352fc2e3-10d1-5d70-be93-5831359ed522" :question/math-academy-id "q-139527" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $11,$ $9,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $11,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=11\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{11}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8c64eaf9-aef9-5ec3-ab69-6953be3e0926"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8c64eaf9-aef9-5ec3-ab69-6953be3e0926" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c64eaf9-aef9-5ec3-ab69-6953be3e0926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7060a51a-f1ac-56cc-8649-47d79f80e66a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a57ac798-2d4f-569f-8786-634addc82e29" "answer-83008559-0106-50b8-aade-d8999902ca44" "answer-9a03575c-0bbb-5f48-9976-7364f8857c77" "answer-743c9f6c-4604-5a5e-ae09-e46410e9b736" "answer-7060a51a-f1ac-56cc-8649-47d79f80e66a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a57ac798-2d4f-569f-8786-634addc82e29" :answer/id #uuid "a57ac798-2d4f-569f-8786-634addc82e29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83008559-0106-50b8-aade-d8999902ca44" :answer/id #uuid "83008559-0106-50b8-aade-d8999902ca44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a03575c-0bbb-5f48-9976-7364f8857c77" :answer/id #uuid "9a03575c-0bbb-5f48-9976-7364f8857c77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-743c9f6c-4604-5a5e-ae09-e46410e9b736" :answer/id #uuid "743c9f6c-4604-5a5e-ae09-e46410e9b736" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7060a51a-f1ac-56cc-8649-47d79f80e66a" :answer/id #uuid "7060a51a-f1ac-56cc-8649-47d79f80e66a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133906 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3a651338-245e-55fb-922d-e222a82f10f8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3a651338-245e-55fb-922d-e222a82f10f8" :question/id #uuid "3a651338-245e-55fb-922d-e222a82f10f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-139756" :question/problem "$$\nR\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}={Q}^{T}\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ R\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $x$ and $b.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6622d779-3fd9-5b54-a00c-38dcd286a099"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6622d779-3fd9-5b54-a00c-38dcd286a099" :answer-field/id #uuid "6622d779-3fd9-5b54-a00c-38dcd286a099" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9c764c56-25d0-593c-92f7-e9e4fd0a3e58" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3ffdba13-f929-50d8-9385-60c71eff301c" "answer-9fc39c01-1abf-555c-aef2-c1808d14ae4d" "answer-0ed48f6f-e99b-522f-906d-c9d987201207" "answer-9c764c56-25d0-593c-92f7-e9e4fd0a3e58" "answer-ad28b9a6-f414-50ef-8499-01767cce2b6d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ffdba13-f929-50d8-9385-60c71eff301c" :answer/id #uuid "3ffdba13-f929-50d8-9385-60c71eff301c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{‖b‖}\\cdot b$ and $x$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fc39c01-1abf-555c-aef2-c1808d14ae4d" :answer/id #uuid "9fc39c01-1abf-555c-aef2-c1808d14ae4d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$b$ and $x$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ed48f6f-e99b-522f-906d-c9d987201207" :answer/id #uuid "0ed48f6f-e99b-522f-906d-c9d987201207" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ and $\\frac{1}{‖b‖}\\cdot b$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c764c56-25d0-593c-92f7-e9e4fd0a3e58" :answer/id #uuid "9c764c56-25d0-593c-92f7-e9e4fd0a3e58" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ and $b$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad28b9a6-f414-50ef-8499-01767cce2b6d" :answer/id #uuid "ad28b9a6-f414-50ef-8499-01767cce2b6d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x$ and ${b}^{T}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-17dbb386-44d9-5210-bf60-77858d50ba0a" :activity/id #uuid "17dbb386-44d9-5210-bf60-77858d50ba0a" :activity/title "Quiz 2" :activity/type :activity.type/assessment :activity/course 17592186046669 :activity/steps ["step-00b2cd0a-8e39-5570-a66f-846996a620a2" "step-eea95270-e69a-5fe8-849f-f648b5219654" "step-610730d0-1f59-5145-b8bb-4fa949bc8411" "step-7d0748c5-6f3f-54e5-bedc-650e9a0c226c" "step-16892fec-26dd-5ef4-928e-f25b0200b0cd" "step-2bd58ec2-5225-5892-bfc1-4f2e5bb66981" "step-ff672a5b-c045-500c-a37b-ad7bc4d837da"] :activity/first-step "step-00b2cd0a-8e39-5570-a66f-846996a620a2" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-00b2cd0a-8e39-5570-a66f-846996a620a2" :step/id #uuid "00b2cd0a-8e39-5570-a66f-846996a620a2" :step/content "question-352fc2e3-10d1-5d70-be93-5831359ed522" :step/next "step-eea95270-e69a-5fe8-849f-f648b5219654" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-eea95270-e69a-5fe8-849f-f648b5219654" :step/id #uuid "eea95270-e69a-5fe8-849f-f648b5219654" :step/content "question-3a651338-245e-55fb-922d-e222a82f10f8" :step/next "step-610730d0-1f59-5145-b8bb-4fa949bc8411" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-610730d0-1f59-5145-b8bb-4fa949bc8411" :step/id #uuid "610730d0-1f59-5145-b8bb-4fa949bc8411" :step/content 17592186395045 :step/next "step-7d0748c5-6f3f-54e5-bedc-650e9a0c226c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7d0748c5-6f3f-54e5-bedc-650e9a0c226c" :step/id #uuid "7d0748c5-6f3f-54e5-bedc-650e9a0c226c" :step/content 17592186555850 :step/next "step-16892fec-26dd-5ef4-928e-f25b0200b0cd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-16892fec-26dd-5ef4-928e-f25b0200b0cd" :step/id #uuid "16892fec-26dd-5ef4-928e-f25b0200b0cd" :step/content 17592186329741 :step/next "step-2bd58ec2-5225-5892-bfc1-4f2e5bb66981" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2bd58ec2-5225-5892-bfc1-4f2e5bb66981" :step/id #uuid "2bd58ec2-5225-5892-bfc1-4f2e5bb66981" :step/content 17592186240978 :step/next "step-ff672a5b-c045-500c-a37b-ad7bc4d837da" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ff672a5b-c045-500c-a37b-ad7bc4d837da" :step/id #uuid "ff672a5b-c045-500c-a37b-ad7bc4d837da" :step/content 17592186442982 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176078 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cb89cc75-091f-5263-a465-e0004e0e6c85" "question-99714685-f61c-5dfd-b220-40d2fda79ac5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cb89cc75-091f-5263-a465-e0004e0e6c85" :question/id #uuid "cb89cc75-091f-5263-a465-e0004e0e6c85" :question/math-academy-id "q-52004" :question/problem "For a fixed temperature, the pressure $P$ of a gas varies inversely with the volume $V$ that it occupies. When $V=3\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ the pressure is $P=2$ megapascal ($\\text{MPa}$). What will be the pressure if the gas is compressed to a container with volume $V=2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "The pressure $P$ is inversely proportional to the volume $V,$ so we have\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{k}{V},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the volume is $V=3\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ the pressure is $P=2$ megapascal ($\\text{MPa}$). We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{k}{3} \\\\ 2\\cdot 3 & =k \\\\ 6 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{6}{V}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the pressure when the volume is $V=2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{6}{2}=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pressure of the gas when $V=2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}$ is $3\\,\\text{MPa}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-877db9e7-19c4-5bae-a15a-39d1105acc5d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-877db9e7-19c4-5bae-a15a-39d1105acc5d" :answer-field/id #uuid "877db9e7-19c4-5bae-a15a-39d1105acc5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-361e105c-8058-5ba1-9a25-7614fd9ff325" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bf7be9cd-c53f-5c97-8fcf-87c050464c14" "answer-d0d2f2e3-319f-50d1-9b74-972ccee98498" "answer-56b0dd7b-330f-52d2-a1ec-732c410bb6b7" "answer-361e105c-8058-5ba1-9a25-7614fd9ff325" "answer-ad0ab94e-ac52-57ec-8d6d-3a909eaf246b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf7be9cd-c53f-5c97-8fcf-87c050464c14" :answer/id #uuid "bf7be9cd-c53f-5c97-8fcf-87c050464c14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{MPa}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0d2f2e3-319f-50d1-9b74-972ccee98498" :answer/id #uuid "d0d2f2e3-319f-50d1-9b74-972ccee98498" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{MPa}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56b0dd7b-330f-52d2-a1ec-732c410bb6b7" :answer/id #uuid "56b0dd7b-330f-52d2-a1ec-732c410bb6b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.8\\,\\text{MPa}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-361e105c-8058-5ba1-9a25-7614fd9ff325" :answer/id #uuid "361e105c-8058-5ba1-9a25-7614fd9ff325" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,\\text{MPa}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad0ab94e-ac52-57ec-8d6d-3a909eaf246b" :answer/id #uuid "ad0ab94e-ac52-57ec-8d6d-3a909eaf246b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,\\text{MPa}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176014 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f5ab26d2-91d7-599f-b642-d61eec8ddc94" "question-04117d3f-552c-54f2-b117-fd9ff4546b57" "question-1ed2e1e9-66f4-55a8-9c62-03d8b636c4ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f5ab26d2-91d7-599f-b642-d61eec8ddc94" :question/id #uuid "f5ab26d2-91d7-599f-b642-d61eec8ddc94" :question/math-academy-id "q-116315" :question/problem "The intensity $I$ of light, measured in $\\text{lux},$ is inversely proportional to the **square** of the distance $d,$ measured in meters, between the light source and the object being illuminated. A device that measures the light intensity is $12$ meters away from the light source and registers $4$ $\\text{lux}.$  What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $d?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the intensity $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the distance $d,$ we must have\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $I=4\\,\\text{lux}$ when $d=12\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =\\frac{k}{{12}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{k}{144} \\\\ 4\\cdot 144 & =k \\\\ 576 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{576}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9d87431e-d6a3-5dbb-a0b5-cd9dae356e8a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9d87431e-d6a3-5dbb-a0b5-cd9dae356e8a" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d87431e-d6a3-5dbb-a0b5-cd9dae356e8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f5e5ce6b-2d1b-5bed-83b2-dcc16add4837" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a7837106-1589-57d2-b906-4c9c68e0e446" "answer-d1c463c3-c064-5991-b54e-dd3508c5031c" "answer-f3366ca9-4710-56bf-af0a-6fb29a726281" "answer-f1b921b1-ed1b-5178-8c66-9eaae69ad95c" "answer-f5e5ce6b-2d1b-5bed-83b2-dcc16add4837"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7837106-1589-57d2-b906-4c9c68e0e446" :answer/id #uuid "a7837106-1589-57d2-b906-4c9c68e0e446" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{525}{{d}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1c463c3-c064-5991-b54e-dd3508c5031c" :answer/id #uuid "d1c463c3-c064-5991-b54e-dd3508c5031c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{526}{{d}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3366ca9-4710-56bf-af0a-6fb29a726281" :answer/id #uuid "f3366ca9-4710-56bf-af0a-6fb29a726281" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{539}{{d}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1b921b1-ed1b-5178-8c66-9eaae69ad95c" :answer/id #uuid "f1b921b1-ed1b-5178-8c66-9eaae69ad95c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{572}{{d}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5e5ce6b-2d1b-5bed-83b2-dcc16add4837" :answer/id #uuid "f5e5ce6b-2d1b-5bed-83b2-dcc16add4837" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{576}{{d}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-04117d3f-552c-54f2-b117-fd9ff4546b57" :question/id #uuid "04117d3f-552c-54f2-b117-fd9ff4546b57" :question/math-academy-id "q-116321" :question/problem "The irradiance $I,$ in $W/{m}^{2},$ of a lamp placed in the center of a spherical chamber varies inversely with the **square** of the radius $r$ of the spherical chamber, measured in meters. It is known that the irradiance is $0.6\\,W/{m}^{2}$ when the radius of the chamber is $3\\,m.$ What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the irradiance $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the radius of the spherical chamber, $r$, we must have\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $I=0.6\\,W/{m}^{2}$ when $r=3\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.6 & =\\frac{k}{{3}^{2}} \\\\ 0.6 & =\\frac{k}{9} \\\\ 0.6\\cdot 9 & =k \\\\ 5.4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{5.4}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bbbea0b5-e068-501a-85b9-14ebd116cadd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bbbea0b5-e068-501a-85b9-14ebd116cadd" :answer-field/id #uuid "bbbea0b5-e068-501a-85b9-14ebd116cadd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2f8017c5-3642-5551-85b5-caf0dc92835a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-efc38971-1f12-5920-bd35-8861d39777a9" "answer-2f8017c5-3642-5551-85b5-caf0dc92835a" "answer-c9aa16ec-6b8c-5aca-9762-25ee61f4aec4" "answer-84571adf-8d7e-51a8-aab6-154b6d0b6347" "answer-4847e4fc-ab7b-5fed-acce-013c3bfb352e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efc38971-1f12-5920-bd35-8861d39777a9" :answer/id #uuid "efc38971-1f12-5920-bd35-8861d39777a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{4.8}{{r}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f8017c5-3642-5551-85b5-caf0dc92835a" :answer/id #uuid "2f8017c5-3642-5551-85b5-caf0dc92835a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{5.4}{{r}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9aa16ec-6b8c-5aca-9762-25ee61f4aec4" :answer/id #uuid "c9aa16ec-6b8c-5aca-9762-25ee61f4aec4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{5.8}{{r}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84571adf-8d7e-51a8-aab6-154b6d0b6347" :answer/id #uuid "84571adf-8d7e-51a8-aab6-154b6d0b6347" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{6.3}{{r}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4847e4fc-ab7b-5fed-acce-013c3bfb352e" :answer/id #uuid "4847e4fc-ab7b-5fed-acce-013c3bfb352e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{6.7}{{r}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1ed2e1e9-66f4-55a8-9c62-03d8b636c4ac" :question/id #uuid "1ed2e1e9-66f4-55a8-9c62-03d8b636c4ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-116337" :question/problem "The intensity of radiation $I,$ in milliroentgen/hour ($\\text{mR}/\\text{h}$), spreading out from an X-ray device is inversely proportional to the **square** of the distance $d,$ measured in meters, between the device and the object being radiated. If a device that measures the radiation intensity registers $82\\,\\text{mR}/\\text{h}$ at a distance of $5\\,\\text{m}.$ What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $d?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the intensity $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the distance $d,$ we must have\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $82\\,\\text{mR}/\\text{h}$ when $d=5\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}82 & =\\frac{k}{{5}^{2}} \\\\ 82 & =\\frac{k}{25} \\\\ 82\\cdot 25 & =k \\\\ 2\\,050 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{2\\,050}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-247bcca9-98a8-5db3-bb40-72912974c475"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-247bcca9-98a8-5db3-bb40-72912974c475" :answer-field/id #uuid "247bcca9-98a8-5db3-bb40-72912974c475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-57c4c63f-2fb5-5791-a8d2-3e2f8df27b27" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eeb4a91f-d653-5fd6-98e4-36fe04cd33cf" "answer-fa966bfa-a7f5-5c04-be74-6a02a6f44a11" "answer-57c4c63f-2fb5-5791-a8d2-3e2f8df27b27" "answer-9060ad28-322c-5fd6-a579-21223cdc86b8" "answer-55d8ec15-bec9-5145-b56e-63807063355c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eeb4a91f-d653-5fd6-98e4-36fe04cd33cf" :answer/id #uuid "eeb4a91f-d653-5fd6-98e4-36fe04cd33cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{1\\,630}{{d}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa966bfa-a7f5-5c04-be74-6a02a6f44a11" :answer/id #uuid "fa966bfa-a7f5-5c04-be74-6a02a6f44a11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{1\\,850}{{d}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57c4c63f-2fb5-5791-a8d2-3e2f8df27b27" :answer/id #uuid "57c4c63f-2fb5-5791-a8d2-3e2f8df27b27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{2\\,050}{{d}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9060ad28-322c-5fd6-a579-21223cdc86b8" :answer/id #uuid "9060ad28-322c-5fd6-a579-21223cdc86b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{2\\,350}{{d}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55d8ec15-bec9-5145-b56e-63807063355c" :answer/id #uuid "55d8ec15-bec9-5145-b56e-63807063355c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "I=\\frac{2\\,850}{{d}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-99714685-f61c-5dfd-b220-40d2fda79ac5" :question/id #uuid "99714685-f61c-5dfd-b220-40d2fda79ac5" :question/math-academy-id "q-120409" :question/problem "The time $t$, in minutes ($min$), it takes for an ice cream to melt is inversely proportional to the environment's temperature $T,$ in degrees Celsius (${}^{∘}C$). If it takes $10\\,min$ for an ice cream to melt at  ${30}^{∘}C,$ how much time it takes for the ice cream to melt at ${20}^{∘}C?$" :question/worked-solution "The time of melting $t$ is inversely proportional to the temperature $T,$ so we have\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{k}{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that it takes $10\\,min$ for an ice cream to melt at ${30}^{∘}C.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{k}{30} \\\\ 10\\cdot 30 & =k \\\\ 300 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{300}{T}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the time it takes for the ice cream to melt at ${20}^{∘}C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{300}{20}=15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time it takes for the ice cream to melt when $T={20}^{∘}C$ is $t=15\\,min.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fb3d5956-200b-5c5c-9bbb-4da333869f74"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fb3d5956-200b-5c5c-9bbb-4da333869f74" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb3d5956-200b-5c5c-9bbb-4da333869f74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ef253e76-101f-5feb-aebb-cc60e77dcb17" :answer-field/choices ["answer-331be38b-9e6c-5e90-9477-39943ce1f0a2" "answer-ef253e76-101f-5feb-aebb-cc60e77dcb17" "answer-65a769b0-ef43-5a92-8d94-9f93a82f87e4" "answer-dfdce043-b80d-5e59-9eaf-4eed1add27d2" "answer-0f96646a-20b2-54f6-ac4d-afa9fbd983c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-331be38b-9e6c-5e90-9477-39943ce1f0a2" :answer/id #uuid "331be38b-9e6c-5e90-9477-39943ce1f0a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13\\,min" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef253e76-101f-5feb-aebb-cc60e77dcb17" :answer/id #uuid "ef253e76-101f-5feb-aebb-cc60e77dcb17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\,min" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65a769b0-ef43-5a92-8d94-9f93a82f87e4" :answer/id #uuid "65a769b0-ef43-5a92-8d94-9f93a82f87e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20\\,min" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfdce043-b80d-5e59-9eaf-4eed1add27d2" :answer/id #uuid "dfdce043-b80d-5e59-9eaf-4eed1add27d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25\\,min" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f96646a-20b2-54f6-ac4d-afa9fbd983c5" :answer/id #uuid "0f96646a-20b2-54f6-ac4d-afa9fbd983c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30\\,min" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f61c7a57-02b3-5f4f-b843-60cea6ec4bde" :activity/id #uuid "f61c7a57-02b3-5f4f-b843-60cea6ec4bde" :activity/title "Modeling With Inverse Variation" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049746 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-ac02dbdc-cd9f-55e1-8008-da2bd4772a77" "step-3811cab8-bfed-5a2b-a00d-bd53f4bc212a" "step-6195b08a-b671-5086-8dde-dbc84452e8ba" "step-55e23826-9116-5685-8d98-1930337eae6d" "step-2c17c573-86f7-52cc-9a73-40fbc246029e"] :activity/first-step "step-ac02dbdc-cd9f-55e1-8008-da2bd4772a77" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-ac02dbdc-cd9f-55e1-8008-da2bd4772a77" :step/id #uuid "ac02dbdc-cd9f-55e1-8008-da2bd4772a77" :step/content "question-cb89cc75-091f-5263-a465-e0004e0e6c85" :step/next "step-3811cab8-bfed-5a2b-a00d-bd53f4bc212a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3811cab8-bfed-5a2b-a00d-bd53f4bc212a" :step/id #uuid "3811cab8-bfed-5a2b-a00d-bd53f4bc212a" :step/content "question-f5ab26d2-91d7-599f-b642-d61eec8ddc94" :step/next "step-6195b08a-b671-5086-8dde-dbc84452e8ba" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6195b08a-b671-5086-8dde-dbc84452e8ba" :step/id #uuid "6195b08a-b671-5086-8dde-dbc84452e8ba" :step/content "question-04117d3f-552c-54f2-b117-fd9ff4546b57" :step/next "step-55e23826-9116-5685-8d98-1930337eae6d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-55e23826-9116-5685-8d98-1930337eae6d" :step/id #uuid "55e23826-9116-5685-8d98-1930337eae6d" :step/content "question-1ed2e1e9-66f4-55a8-9c62-03d8b636c4ac" :step/next "step-2c17c573-86f7-52cc-9a73-40fbc246029e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2c17c573-86f7-52cc-9a73-40fbc246029e" :step/id #uuid "2c17c573-86f7-52cc-9a73-40fbc246029e" :step/content "question-99714685-f61c-5dfd-b220-40d2fda79ac5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-210e4b47-1e0a-56be-aa39-497b4081eae6" "question-3fb92bee-a19d-5089-88c2-ed5488b77548"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-210e4b47-1e0a-56be-aa39-497b4081eae6" :question/id #uuid "210e4b47-1e0a-56be-aa39-497b4081eae6" :question/math-academy-id "q-95404" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is diagonalizable\n\nII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u=\\alpha v$ for some scalar $\\alpha $\n\nIII. $A$ might have a complex eigenvalue" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, so, $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. So if the two eigenvalues correspond to different eigenvalues, we cannot have $u=\\alpha v$ for some scalar $\\alpha .$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8e5650dd-916f-5b6f-b0e5-9eadfbe79952"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8e5650dd-916f-5b6f-b0e5-9eadfbe79952" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e5650dd-916f-5b6f-b0e5-9eadfbe79952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2ba00610-5038-5e8c-b108-a154138d8fd5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-367c0855-5233-510b-8d48-efa4737d7993" "answer-f058d428-0146-5872-86b8-f12ac8d0a722" "answer-2ba00610-5038-5e8c-b108-a154138d8fd5" "answer-ad12ed5f-34ad-5ed9-9497-1c2dbe2c1d2e" "answer-03447015-8a69-5cc9-8dc8-414b596017be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-367c0855-5233-510b-8d48-efa4737d7993" :answer/id #uuid "367c0855-5233-510b-8d48-efa4737d7993" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f058d428-0146-5872-86b8-f12ac8d0a722" :answer/id #uuid "f058d428-0146-5872-86b8-f12ac8d0a722" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ba00610-5038-5e8c-b108-a154138d8fd5" :answer/id #uuid "2ba00610-5038-5e8c-b108-a154138d8fd5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad12ed5f-34ad-5ed9-9497-1c2dbe2c1d2e" :answer/id #uuid "ad12ed5f-34ad-5ed9-9497-1c2dbe2c1d2e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03447015-8a69-5cc9-8dc8-414b596017be" :answer/id #uuid "03447015-8a69-5cc9-8dc8-414b596017be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3fb92bee-a19d-5089-88c2-ed5488b77548" :question/id #uuid "3fb92bee-a19d-5089-88c2-ed5488b77548" :question/math-academy-id "q-95410" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$\n\nII. $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable\n\nIII. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-69b45667-9a66-586d-aafa-6664e3584267"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-69b45667-9a66-586d-aafa-6664e3584267" :answer-field/id #uuid "69b45667-9a66-586d-aafa-6664e3584267" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-72c03a73-aa07-54e2-995b-498454ec7b39" :answer-field/choices ["answer-28767abe-e741-5825-bba9-64081ca06ad7" "answer-3c608fb3-267f-554d-8bf6-edba88e5c867" "answer-72c03a73-aa07-54e2-995b-498454ec7b39" "answer-3d68c016-caf8-513d-b3a9-2868a44187fd" "answer-3c80624a-5983-591d-a673-ae4cac20ce07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28767abe-e741-5825-bba9-64081ca06ad7" :answer/id #uuid "28767abe-e741-5825-bba9-64081ca06ad7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c608fb3-267f-554d-8bf6-edba88e5c867" :answer/id #uuid "3c608fb3-267f-554d-8bf6-edba88e5c867" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72c03a73-aa07-54e2-995b-498454ec7b39" :answer/id #uuid "72c03a73-aa07-54e2-995b-498454ec7b39" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d68c016-caf8-513d-b3a9-2868a44187fd" :answer/id #uuid "3d68c016-caf8-513d-b3a9-2868a44187fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c80624a-5983-591d-a673-ae4cac20ce07" :answer/id #uuid "3c80624a-5983-591d-a673-ae4cac20ce07" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-35aadbd5-d57a-54cd-9aee-32da4b42c514" :activity/id #uuid "35aadbd5-d57a-54cd-9aee-32da4b42c514" :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049849 :activity/course 17592186046669 :activity/steps ["step-1d29f179-63a2-5b26-9010-60a5f5cccbf2" "step-00649106-db72-5f98-a26b-cf0c8648ce5c" "step-e00bef3d-8e9a-54a7-b8b3-0f454cf1dc92" "step-4b5a9152-1cd3-5945-ba4f-3e6468f968f3" "step-8ac724bf-e8ba-5a05-9ed3-888527594f6d"] :activity/first-step "step-1d29f179-63a2-5b26-9010-60a5f5cccbf2" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1d29f179-63a2-5b26-9010-60a5f5cccbf2" :step/id #uuid "1d29f179-63a2-5b26-9010-60a5f5cccbf2" :step/content "question-210e4b47-1e0a-56be-aa39-497b4081eae6" :step/next "step-00649106-db72-5f98-a26b-cf0c8648ce5c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-00649106-db72-5f98-a26b-cf0c8648ce5c" :step/id #uuid "00649106-db72-5f98-a26b-cf0c8648ce5c" :step/content 17592186366636 :step/next "step-e00bef3d-8e9a-54a7-b8b3-0f454cf1dc92" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e00bef3d-8e9a-54a7-b8b3-0f454cf1dc92" :step/id #uuid "e00bef3d-8e9a-54a7-b8b3-0f454cf1dc92" :step/content 17592186411856 :step/next "step-4b5a9152-1cd3-5945-ba4f-3e6468f968f3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4b5a9152-1cd3-5945-ba4f-3e6468f968f3" :step/id #uuid "4b5a9152-1cd3-5945-ba4f-3e6468f968f3" :step/content "question-3fb92bee-a19d-5089-88c2-ed5488b77548" :step/next "step-8ac724bf-e8ba-5a05-9ed3-888527594f6d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8ac724bf-e8ba-5a05-9ed3-888527594f6d" :step/id #uuid "8ac724bf-e8ba-5a05-9ed3-888527594f6d" :step/content 17592186366650 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-81cebe4e-1d65-5357-b789-c0fc16951ab5" "question-e986899c-1ec0-569d-94ba-d6811b53879c" "question-c04c2ae6-0944-57e5-bf4e-7441e7fccca7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-81cebe4e-1d65-5357-b789-c0fc16951ab5" :question/id #uuid "81cebe4e-1d65-5357-b789-c0fc16951ab5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38040" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x+3)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-8x+3$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-x-3$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x-2$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=-3$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-3$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =2(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-8(-3)+3 \\\\  & =2(-27)+3(9)+24+3 \\\\  & =-54+27+24+3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-3$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-(-3)-3 \\\\  & =-27+3(9)+3-3 \\\\  & =-27+27+3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-3$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+2(-3)^{2}-(-3)-2 \\\\  & =-27+2(9)+3-2 \\\\  & =-27+18+3-2 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f84d9338-e966-5479-8801-6e8f15658f84"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f84d9338-e966-5479-8801-6e8f15658f84" :answer-field/id #uuid "f84d9338-e966-5479-8801-6e8f15658f84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-35e82de2-f5a4-5a17-8312-4f73519d2e6f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35e82de2-f5a4-5a17-8312-4f73519d2e6f" "answer-47ca42e7-e9b5-518e-9713-80c7cee79696" "answer-9e82d34b-fe77-5fe5-9e29-97290fc018c9" "answer-c527e597-42e6-5d49-9d63-cfb389529772" "answer-fec4a528-b0f6-5c07-bfc6-11532012aef8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35e82de2-f5a4-5a17-8312-4f73519d2e6f" :answer/id #uuid "35e82de2-f5a4-5a17-8312-4f73519d2e6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47ca42e7-e9b5-518e-9713-80c7cee79696" :answer/id #uuid "47ca42e7-e9b5-518e-9713-80c7cee79696" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e82d34b-fe77-5fe5-9e29-97290fc018c9" :answer/id #uuid "9e82d34b-fe77-5fe5-9e29-97290fc018c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c527e597-42e6-5d49-9d63-cfb389529772" :answer/id #uuid "c527e597-42e6-5d49-9d63-cfb389529772" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fec4a528-b0f6-5c07-bfc6-11532012aef8" :answer/id #uuid "fec4a528-b0f6-5c07-bfc6-11532012aef8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e986899c-1ec0-569d-94ba-d6811b53879c" :question/id #uuid "e986899c-1ec0-569d-94ba-d6811b53879c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73779" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x-2)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{3}-4x$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}+4x+5$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{2}-4x+4$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=2$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=2$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=2$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & ={2}^{3}-4(2) \\\\  & =8-8 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=2$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={2}^{2}+4(2)+5 \\\\  & =4+8+5 \\\\  & =17\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=2$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(2) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+4 \\\\  & =4-8+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only .\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c70a0b59-1c4a-5aa7-bb33-d1a6a216cf95"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c70a0b59-1c4a-5aa7-bb33-d1a6a216cf95" :answer-field/id #uuid "c70a0b59-1c4a-5aa7-bb33-d1a6a216cf95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f02ef9f3-2866-5904-be26-7b7de039fb1c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f02ef9f3-2866-5904-be26-7b7de039fb1c" "answer-c845ff5b-3e9e-5056-902c-a2214e1aa90b" "answer-c2c79867-73d6-5cc0-a3af-25cf7ccb5cd1" "answer-fce13d87-4fc4-577a-a664-6cc3884100f5" "answer-d069d041-5456-57d8-b6c8-da7c7f8df610"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f02ef9f3-2866-5904-be26-7b7de039fb1c" :answer/id #uuid "f02ef9f3-2866-5904-be26-7b7de039fb1c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c845ff5b-3e9e-5056-902c-a2214e1aa90b" :answer/id #uuid "c845ff5b-3e9e-5056-902c-a2214e1aa90b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2c79867-73d6-5cc0-a3af-25cf7ccb5cd1" :answer/id #uuid "c2c79867-73d6-5cc0-a3af-25cf7ccb5cd1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fce13d87-4fc4-577a-a664-6cc3884100f5" :answer/id #uuid "fce13d87-4fc4-577a-a664-6cc3884100f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d069d041-5456-57d8-b6c8-da7c7f8df610" :answer/id #uuid "d069d041-5456-57d8-b6c8-da7c7f8df610" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087756 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-50aaea5b-0826-518a-8a27-2b0395848ac8" "question-cef78ed2-9af9-556a-8a9d-05a7101331ab"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-50aaea5b-0826-518a-8a27-2b0395848ac8" :question/id #uuid "50aaea5b-0826-518a-8a27-2b0395848ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73789" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-3x-2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(3x+2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+2{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-3(-\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =3(-\\frac{8}{27})+2(\\frac{4}{9})+3(\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{9}+\\frac{8}{9}+2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6e9c46f9-73fa-5bae-8ba5-d402d0aa2e48"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6e9c46f9-73fa-5bae-8ba5-d402d0aa2e48" :answer-field/id #uuid "6e9c46f9-73fa-5bae-8ba5-d402d0aa2e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-80f25a70-a063-50e7-b4ec-df4df125ef08" :answer-field/choices ["answer-016050f4-b327-51eb-ac59-6cdb4f2d394b" "answer-cc39eb67-2c40-56a3-bfba-b0087f7d23c8" "answer-80f25a70-a063-50e7-b4ec-df4df125ef08" "answer-c1a088d6-83f2-526a-b6ad-dd5f6c749f89" "answer-1649ec6b-8d70-5b6e-8662-99e140405802"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-016050f4-b327-51eb-ac59-6cdb4f2d394b" :answer/id #uuid "016050f4-b327-51eb-ac59-6cdb4f2d394b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc39eb67-2c40-56a3-bfba-b0087f7d23c8" :answer/id #uuid "cc39eb67-2c40-56a3-bfba-b0087f7d23c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80f25a70-a063-50e7-b4ec-df4df125ef08" :answer/id #uuid "80f25a70-a063-50e7-b4ec-df4df125ef08" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1a088d6-83f2-526a-b6ad-dd5f6c749f89" :answer/id #uuid "c1a088d6-83f2-526a-b6ad-dd5f6c749f89" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1649ec6b-8d70-5b6e-8662-99e140405802" :answer/id #uuid "1649ec6b-8d70-5b6e-8662-99e140405802" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c04c2ae6-0944-57e5-bf4e-7441e7fccca7" :question/id #uuid "c04c2ae6-0944-57e5-bf4e-7441e7fccca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-112376" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+x-6?$\n\nI. $x=3$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=3$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ={3}^{2}+(3)-6 \\\\  & =9+3-6 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=3$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x-3)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x-3)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d4b18df5-7e14-5ba9-ad19-fa824c06c4c6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d4b18df5-7e14-5ba9-ad19-fa824c06c4c6" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4b18df5-7e14-5ba9-ad19-fa824c06c4c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e8cb940e-d44e-5a8f-988a-50c419452c79" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9f06c2d4-90c4-5f39-a2ec-6d108a9826a4" "answer-2e2007b5-5f0d-5777-a12b-059ac6926d9f" "answer-a94df82a-733b-5f27-86b0-983a28681f0d" "answer-1e583ae8-d9fe-52a9-b929-554bdbe54145" "answer-e8cb940e-d44e-5a8f-988a-50c419452c79"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f06c2d4-90c4-5f39-a2ec-6d108a9826a4" :answer/id #uuid "9f06c2d4-90c4-5f39-a2ec-6d108a9826a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e2007b5-5f0d-5777-a12b-059ac6926d9f" :answer/id #uuid "2e2007b5-5f0d-5777-a12b-059ac6926d9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a94df82a-733b-5f27-86b0-983a28681f0d" :answer/id #uuid "a94df82a-733b-5f27-86b0-983a28681f0d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e583ae8-d9fe-52a9-b929-554bdbe54145" :answer/id #uuid "1e583ae8-d9fe-52a9-b929-554bdbe54145" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8cb940e-d44e-5a8f-988a-50c419452c79" :answer/id #uuid "e8cb940e-d44e-5a8f-988a-50c419452c79" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cef78ed2-9af9-556a-8a9d-05a7101331ab" :question/id #uuid "cef78ed2-9af9-556a-8a9d-05a7101331ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-112468" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-3x+2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(2x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(2x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(2x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{1}{2}) & ={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}-3(-\\frac{1}{2})+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8508e80e-2237-5e61-952e-da76d1a6dd3a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8508e80e-2237-5e61-952e-da76d1a6dd3a" :answer-field/id #uuid "8508e80e-2237-5e61-952e-da76d1a6dd3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7215867e-586e-537b-aa3c-c7a87d387e5d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a0014fe0-63da-51e9-bf6d-ad45b394652b" "answer-7b90dcb3-20f2-5889-885c-583181c9f4e6" "answer-fedcbdd6-f4a6-5750-a08c-d1f7b5c28221" "answer-50a01771-cde1-54b6-903b-5be07bb31b68" "answer-7215867e-586e-537b-aa3c-c7a87d387e5d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0014fe0-63da-51e9-bf6d-ad45b394652b" :answer/id #uuid "a0014fe0-63da-51e9-bf6d-ad45b394652b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b90dcb3-20f2-5889-885c-583181c9f4e6" :answer/id #uuid "7b90dcb3-20f2-5889-885c-583181c9f4e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fedcbdd6-f4a6-5750-a08c-d1f7b5c28221" :answer/id #uuid "fedcbdd6-f4a6-5750-a08c-d1f7b5c28221" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50a01771-cde1-54b6-903b-5be07bb31b68" :answer/id #uuid "50a01771-cde1-54b6-903b-5be07bb31b68" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7215867e-586e-537b-aa3c-c7a87d387e5d" :answer/id #uuid "7215867e-586e-537b-aa3c-c7a87d387e5d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-17431833-f890-5a9b-bd60-79825d278655" :activity/id #uuid "17431833-f890-5a9b-bd60-79825d278655" :activity/title "The Factor Theorem" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049242 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-493d11f3-2102-562a-b794-a790e09ff1be" "step-05656f4c-c5e5-5079-9eb0-b865d0bab77a" "step-2c87655d-7944-5ac9-9c29-bd3ab5f7338f" "step-e5a159e3-2dc9-58e6-b01c-29cc364c394c" "step-89659a65-f3c8-5528-aa9b-c7187752ecec"] :activity/first-step "step-493d11f3-2102-562a-b794-a790e09ff1be" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-493d11f3-2102-562a-b794-a790e09ff1be" :step/id #uuid "493d11f3-2102-562a-b794-a790e09ff1be" :step/content "question-81cebe4e-1d65-5357-b789-c0fc16951ab5" :step/next "step-05656f4c-c5e5-5079-9eb0-b865d0bab77a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-05656f4c-c5e5-5079-9eb0-b865d0bab77a" :step/id #uuid "05656f4c-c5e5-5079-9eb0-b865d0bab77a" :step/content "question-e986899c-1ec0-569d-94ba-d6811b53879c" :step/next "step-2c87655d-7944-5ac9-9c29-bd3ab5f7338f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2c87655d-7944-5ac9-9c29-bd3ab5f7338f" :step/id #uuid "2c87655d-7944-5ac9-9c29-bd3ab5f7338f" :step/content "question-50aaea5b-0826-518a-8a27-2b0395848ac8" :step/next "step-e5a159e3-2dc9-58e6-b01c-29cc364c394c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e5a159e3-2dc9-58e6-b01c-29cc364c394c" :step/id #uuid "e5a159e3-2dc9-58e6-b01c-29cc364c394c" :step/content "question-c04c2ae6-0944-57e5-bf4e-7441e7fccca7" :step/next "step-89659a65-f3c8-5528-aa9b-c7187752ecec" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-89659a65-f3c8-5528-aa9b-c7187752ecec" :step/id #uuid "89659a65-f3c8-5528-aa9b-c7187752ecec" :step/content "question-cef78ed2-9af9-556a-8a9d-05a7101331ab" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119836 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c9dd16b9-4054-5136-a332-5517b98207e0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c9dd16b9-4054-5136-a332-5517b98207e0" :question/id #uuid "c9dd16b9-4054-5136-a332-5517b98207e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-33977" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨4,1,6⟩+t⟨2,6,0⟩,\\,Π:r\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩=-32.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's write the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨4+2t,1+6t,6⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨4+2t,1+6t,6⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ & =-32 \\\\ ⟨4+2t,1+6t,6⟩\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ & =-32 \\\\ -24(4+2t)+8(1+6t)+6\\cdot 6 & =-32 \\\\ -96-48t+8+48t+36 & =-32 \\\\ -52 & =-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has no solution. Therefore, we conclude that the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ do not intersect." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fff9c6a5-d7fa-5ed1-9590-bb662eb6e249"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fff9c6a5-d7fa-5ed1-9590-bb662eb6e249" :answer-field/id #uuid "fff9c6a5-d7fa-5ed1-9590-bb662eb6e249" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-54bfb8e5-1bc3-55dc-b6bb-ca377e8ee977" :answer-field/choices ["answer-40fa63da-8d25-5d94-97fe-f363984d351a" "answer-abe12585-4813-5617-a74b-afa58fdb9f12" "answer-b300f230-78be-5357-879b-21a0f3bf43a5" "answer-a0e9bf2c-81b2-536b-98ec-2dd8b4e53ea4" "answer-54bfb8e5-1bc3-55dc-b6bb-ca377e8ee977"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40fa63da-8d25-5d94-97fe-f363984d351a" :answer/id #uuid "40fa63da-8d25-5d94-97fe-f363984d351a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-24,4,6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abe12585-4813-5617-a74b-afa58fdb9f12" :answer/id #uuid "abe12585-4813-5617-a74b-afa58fdb9f12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,1,6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b300f230-78be-5357-879b-21a0f3bf43a5" :answer/id #uuid "b300f230-78be-5357-879b-21a0f3bf43a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,6,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0e9bf2c-81b2-536b-98ec-2dd8b4e53ea4" :answer/id #uuid "a0e9bf2c-81b2-536b-98ec-2dd8b4e53ea4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,1,6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54bfb8e5-1bc3-55dc-b6bb-ca377e8ee977" :answer/id #uuid "54bfb8e5-1bc3-55dc-b6bb-ca377e8ee977" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No intersection" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119838 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-63cc57b7-1fbb-5327-9f12-ff47fb613fec"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-63cc57b7-1fbb-5327-9f12-ff47fb613fec" :question/id #uuid "63cc57b7-1fbb-5327-9f12-ff47fb613fec" :question/math-academy-id "q-73995" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3+t \\\\ y=-1+2t \\\\ z=2+4t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:6x+4y+3z=88.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+4y+3z & =88 \\\\ 6(-3+t)+4(-1+2t)+3(2+4t) & =88 \\\\ -18+6t-4+8t+6+12t & =88 \\\\ 26t & =104 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=4$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3+4=1 \\\\ y=-1+2(4)=7 \\\\ z=2+4(4)=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(1,7,18).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f4196ccd-0e24-51af-ad66-5ad460d2a7e2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f4196ccd-0e24-51af-ad66-5ad460d2a7e2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4196ccd-0e24-51af-ad66-5ad460d2a7e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-98765244-eaa1-58ef-9963-d3e5ae56d7ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8cea1e50-21c3-5c40-9866-c7a6949c9931" "answer-98765244-eaa1-58ef-9963-d3e5ae56d7ed" "answer-2fdae745-6cbf-5dbe-bdd2-ea7547e9e300" "answer-2d0857e4-1338-5c7a-ad65-1684adcb89b5" "answer-df482e11-da20-5e10-9235-bb050ae6ebf4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cea1e50-21c3-5c40-9866-c7a6949c9931" :answer/id #uuid "8cea1e50-21c3-5c40-9866-c7a6949c9931" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,6,8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98765244-eaa1-58ef-9963-d3e5ae56d7ed" :answer/id #uuid "98765244-eaa1-58ef-9963-d3e5ae56d7ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,7,18)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fdae745-6cbf-5dbe-bdd2-ea7547e9e300" :answer/id #uuid "2fdae745-6cbf-5dbe-bdd2-ea7547e9e300" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(10,9,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d0857e4-1338-5c7a-ad65-1684adcb89b5" :answer/id #uuid "2d0857e4-1338-5c7a-ad65-1684adcb89b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(7,-5,12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df482e11-da20-5e10-9235-bb050ae6ebf4" :answer/id #uuid "df482e11-da20-5e10-9235-bb050ae6ebf4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No intersection" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-50c42498-d1ce-5a54-97b7-c0af9956a7d8" :activity/id #uuid "50c42498-d1ce-5a54-97b7-c0af9956a7d8" :activity/title "The Intersection Between a Line and a Plane" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050148 :activity/course 17592186046669 :activity/steps ["step-ae8d1bdd-e6e6-5260-8dba-9c55868390df" "step-17f1e51d-aa7f-5439-b877-0e6bb77db4bf" "step-85bcb97e-37af-5ca8-9d58-81d4ae0c6fbe" "step-2c8a2fa0-140a-5e16-9bdf-6e1de01aec40" "step-d89e8710-7753-5df9-a5a6-86101a7dcce9"] :activity/first-step "step-ae8d1bdd-e6e6-5260-8dba-9c55868390df" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-ae8d1bdd-e6e6-5260-8dba-9c55868390df" :step/id #uuid "ae8d1bdd-e6e6-5260-8dba-9c55868390df" :step/content 17592186304971 :step/next "step-17f1e51d-aa7f-5439-b877-0e6bb77db4bf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-17f1e51d-aa7f-5439-b877-0e6bb77db4bf" :step/id #uuid "17f1e51d-aa7f-5439-b877-0e6bb77db4bf" :step/content "question-c9dd16b9-4054-5136-a332-5517b98207e0" :step/next "step-85bcb97e-37af-5ca8-9d58-81d4ae0c6fbe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-85bcb97e-37af-5ca8-9d58-81d4ae0c6fbe" :step/id #uuid "85bcb97e-37af-5ca8-9d58-81d4ae0c6fbe" :step/content 17592186402256 :step/next "step-2c8a2fa0-140a-5e16-9bdf-6e1de01aec40" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2c8a2fa0-140a-5e16-9bdf-6e1de01aec40" :step/id #uuid "2c8a2fa0-140a-5e16-9bdf-6e1de01aec40" :step/content 17592186484201 :step/next "step-d89e8710-7753-5df9-a5a6-86101a7dcce9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d89e8710-7753-5df9-a5a6-86101a7dcce9" :step/id #uuid "d89e8710-7753-5df9-a5a6-86101a7dcce9" :step/content "question-63cc57b7-1fbb-5327-9f12-ff47fb613fec" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172031 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-03451249-6784-5494-bb32-85da8e4ecff7" "question-5328fc24-dd2c-524e-ade9-b248f99c64bf" "question-f312482e-bbe1-5f40-a816-ce63ddb8cbac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171837 :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,1]?$\n\nI. $p: \\{ 0, 1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}$\n\nII. $q: \\mathbb{R} \\to [ 0, 1 ]$\n\nIII. $r: [0,1] \\to [ 0, 1 )$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172032 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-33ee1a61-aa83-56a5-8209-eec63038ff68" "question-915687d1-7d3d-5688-b25a-20c50fc27b30" "question-e8bbc308-2450-589f-b174-679c413f5f6e" "question-f9c7a6ed-7bc4-5fae-bd01-cf7380224c4e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171838 :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$.\"*\n\nI. $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\nII. $g: Y \\to \\mathbb{Q}$\n\nIII. $g:\\mathbb{R} \\to Y$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172033 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-97bd3e47-4a7d-51fe-87e4-ccb53cfbd7e2" "question-366b227d-9c09-5249-bc0f-cb45c09620a0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171839 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $g(x)=x-x^3?$\n\nI. $g:x \\mapsto x-x^3$\n\nII. $g:x \\mapsto -x^3$\n\nIII. $g:-x \\mapsto x^3$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172034 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-af103ef7-b89b-5289-9d5d-02c1e186f66e" "question-1110805d-d0df-5ae9-af4b-5eeeabf99273"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171840 :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}?$\n\nI. The domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$\n\nII. The codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$\n\nIII. The range of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-03451249-6784-5494-bb32-85da8e4ecff7" :question/id #uuid "03451249-6784-5494-bb32-85da8e4ecff7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96750" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain $(0,2)?$\n\nI. $p:(0,2)\\to \\{0,2\\}$\n\nII. $q:R\\to (0,2)$\n\nIII. $r:(0,2)\\to R$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is $(0,2),$ the interval from $0$ to $2$ (not including $0$ nor $2$).\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $(0,2),$ the interval from $0$ to $2$ (not including $0$ nor $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-57c4d1e2-df8c-582b-885f-5ecfb77ad977"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-57c4d1e2-df8c-582b-885f-5ecfb77ad977" :answer-field/id #uuid "57c4d1e2-df8c-582b-885f-5ecfb77ad977" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-86d8385b-658f-55ab-818a-9655645ea8ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87f28eff-b85c-51e4-bfdb-402ad594d1d7" "answer-86d8385b-658f-55ab-818a-9655645ea8ca" "answer-cef5775c-2fbc-5e17-99c4-f04975560bc1" "answer-4ee7dff7-3395-58fe-8812-401b24d3c95c" "answer-02afc777-71c7-54a7-ba97-7bbbfed64a98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87f28eff-b85c-51e4-bfdb-402ad594d1d7" :answer/id #uuid "87f28eff-b85c-51e4-bfdb-402ad594d1d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86d8385b-658f-55ab-818a-9655645ea8ca" :answer/id #uuid "86d8385b-658f-55ab-818a-9655645ea8ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cef5775c-2fbc-5e17-99c4-f04975560bc1" :answer/id #uuid "cef5775c-2fbc-5e17-99c4-f04975560bc1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ee7dff7-3395-58fe-8812-401b24d3c95c" :answer/id #uuid "4ee7dff7-3395-58fe-8812-401b24d3c95c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02afc777-71c7-54a7-ba97-7bbbfed64a98" :answer/id #uuid "02afc777-71c7-54a7-ba97-7bbbfed64a98" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172627 :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,3)?$\n\nI. $p:\\{0,3\\}\\to R$\n\nII. $q:R\\to [0,3)$\n\nIII. $r:[0,3)\\to \\{0,3\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[0,3),$ the interval from $0$ to $3$ (including $0$ but not including $3$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $\\{0,3\\},$ the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $3$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171387 :answer-field/correct 17592186171392 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-5328fc24-dd2c-524e-ade9-b248f99c64bf" :question/id #uuid "5328fc24-dd2c-524e-ade9-b248f99c64bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-96742" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain $R?$\n\nI. $p:[0,1]\\to R$\n\nII. $q:R\\to R$\n\nIII. $r:[0,1]\\to [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $[0,1],$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (including $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0696edc7-2d9c-5594-b819-83895889d785"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0696edc7-2d9c-5594-b819-83895889d785" :answer-field/id #uuid "0696edc7-2d9c-5594-b819-83895889d785" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-94fa83da-80c7-5f55-9464-39fa5c58c114" :answer-field/choices ["answer-94fa83da-80c7-5f55-9464-39fa5c58c114" "answer-02c9c58a-a214-5eba-901a-bdc452270c65" "answer-c329a7b2-7848-5e75-84a5-25bcb45d6cff" "answer-d81158c2-eba9-59f7-9367-d11127d5d5a0" "answer-8f698421-325f-5fcb-a32f-80af1dd9ee46"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94fa83da-80c7-5f55-9464-39fa5c58c114" :answer/id #uuid "94fa83da-80c7-5f55-9464-39fa5c58c114" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02c9c58a-a214-5eba-901a-bdc452270c65" :answer/id #uuid "02c9c58a-a214-5eba-901a-bdc452270c65" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c329a7b2-7848-5e75-84a5-25bcb45d6cff" :answer/id #uuid "c329a7b2-7848-5e75-84a5-25bcb45d6cff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d81158c2-eba9-59f7-9367-d11127d5d5a0" :answer/id #uuid "d81158c2-eba9-59f7-9367-d11127d5d5a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f698421-325f-5fcb-a32f-80af1dd9ee46" :answer/id #uuid "8f698421-325f-5fcb-a32f-80af1dd9ee46" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f312482e-bbe1-5f40-a816-ce63ddb8cbac" :question/id #uuid "f312482e-bbe1-5f40-a816-ce63ddb8cbac" :question/math-academy-id "q-96746" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain $(-2,0)?$\n\nI. $p:(-2,0)\\to \\{-2,0\\}$\n\nII. $q:(-2,0)\\to (-2,0)$\n\nIII. $r:[-2,0)\\to (0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is $(-2,0),$ the interval from $-2$ to $0$ (not including $-2$ nor $0$).\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $(-2,0),$ the interval from $-2$ to $0$ (not including $-2$ nor $0$).\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $[-2,0),$ the interval from $-2$ to $0$ (including $-2$ but not including $0$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-550f1c25-0293-5be1-ae65-eb50dd02286b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-550f1c25-0293-5be1-ae65-eb50dd02286b" :answer-field/id #uuid "550f1c25-0293-5be1-ae65-eb50dd02286b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3d23e7c9-40ba-5b54-b02d-21430ba708f2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d23e7c9-40ba-5b54-b02d-21430ba708f2" "answer-884b94a9-d6b2-5eeb-b350-6ea73296ca6a" "answer-3e4753ac-ec9a-5118-9d5f-497d9fdf772e" "answer-4b0dea5d-3507-56bb-8583-68afdd27c73c" "answer-64b5696e-2731-5353-9177-5de35368dca6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d23e7c9-40ba